(window.NICHE_PACKS = window.NICHE_PACKS || []).push([
{"chapter":{"id": "cleaning", "name": "Cleaning & Pressure Washing", "icon": "🧽", "color": "#38BDF8", "description": "Clean homes and small offices and pressure wash driveways, patios, siding and decks for people near you.", "group": "local", "breakdown": {"group": "local", "oneLiner": "Clean homes and wash driveways for people nearby", "cost": "Low to medium: a cleaning kit is cheap, a washer costs more", "difficulty": "Easy to start", "pieces": [{"title": "What it is", "body": "A one-person service where you clean homes and small offices, and pressure wash driveways, patios, siding and decks. Customers pay for a finished result and saved time.", "points": ["Home and small-office cleaning", "Driveway, patio, siding and deck washing", "Paid per job or on a repeat schedule"]}, {"title": "Who it's for", "body": "People who are reliable, don't mind physical work and can talk to neighbors. You don't need a degree, but you do need to be trustworthy and careful.", "points": ["Dependable and on time, every time", "Fine with physical, hands-on work", "Comfortable asking for work locally"]}, {"title": "A normal week", "body": "A mix of jobs, estimates and admin: some cleans or washes, a few quotes, photos, messages and bookkeeping. Weather can move outdoor jobs around.", "points": ["Jobs on set days, estimates in between", "Weather can shift outdoor washing days", "An hour or two for messages and books"]}, {"title": "What it costs to start", "body": "Cleaning needs a basic kit. A pressure washer costs more, but you can rent first. Add insurance and any local registration. Costs vary a lot, so get real quotes.", "points": ["Cleaning supplies and a vacuum", "Washer and nozzles, or rent at first", "Insurance, registration and a phone plan"]}, {"title": "Tools you'll use", "body": "Cloths, brushes and cleaners for homes. A pressure washer, hoses, nozzles and a surface cleaner for outdoors. Safety gear, a phone and a calendar.", "points": ["Microfiber cloths, mop and vacuum", "Pressure washer and surface cleaner", "Google Business Profile and a calendar"]}, {"title": "The hard parts", "body": "The work is physical and weather can cancel jobs. The first months are often slow, some customers are picky, and a wrong move can damage property.", "points": ["Physical work and sore muscles", "A slow start while people get to know you", "Damage risk, so test first and get insured"]}]}, "modules": [{"title": "Start here: your first offer", "goal": "Understand both sides of the work, set up simply and legally, and write a narrow first offer you've practiced."}, {"title": "Gear up and work safely", "goal": "Pack the right gear, avoid chemical and surface mistakes, and finish every job the same careful way."}, {"title": "Find and win customers", "goal": "Get your first customers from people nearby, show your work with permission and send estimates that get a yes."}, {"title": "Run it like a business", "goal": "Price jobs with a clear scope and minimum, handle problems, keep simple books and win repeat clients."}], "checklists": [{"title": "Cleaning & Pressure Washing Startup Checklist", "items": ["Pick one side of the business to start with", "Ask your city or county what registration or licenses you need", "Talk to an insurance agent about cover for your work", "Open a separate bank account for the business", "Write your offer: three services or fewer, with what is and isn't included", "Do a timed practice job and take before and after photos", "Set up your free Google Business Profile", "Send your offer to five people you know"]}, {"title": "Before-Every-Job Checklist", "items": ["Confirm the date, address, scope and price in writing", "Ask about pets, parking, access and where the water and power are", "Pack your kit and check every product is in its labeled bottle", "Pack safety gear: goggles, ear protection, gloves, non-slip shoes", "Walk the job with the customer and note damage that was already there", "Test a hidden spot before you pressure wash any new surface", "Take before photos, if the customer has said yes", "Do a final walk-through, take payment and ask about the next booking"]}], "templates": [{"title": "Written Estimate", "category": "Estimate", "body": "Estimate from [Your Business Name]\nPhone: [Your Phone]   Email: [Your Email]\n\nPrepared for: [Customer Name]      Date: [Date]\nJob address: [Address]\n\nService: [e.g. home clean / driveway and walkway wash]\nIncludes: [list each room or surface and each task]\nNot included: [e.g. inside oven, windows, roof, fragile surfaces]\n\nSize or area: [bedrooms and bathrooms / square feet]\nPrice: $[Amount]\nMinimum charge: $[Amount]\nOptional add-ons: [item] $[Amount]; [item] $[Amount]\n\nEstimated time: [X hours]\nProposed date: [Date and time window]\n\nBefore we start, please: [move cars and pets / clear the floors / make sure the outdoor tap and power are reachable].\n\nThis price is based on [photos / a visit on date]. If the job turns out much bigger or dirtier than described, I will tell you before I continue.\n\nThis estimate is valid for [14] days. To book, reply with the day that suits you."}, {"title": "Service Agreement with Photo Permission", "category": "Contract", "body": "Service agreement between [Your Business Name] (\"Provider\") and [Customer Name] (\"Customer\") for work at [Address] on [Date].\n\nScope of work: [description, including what is not included]\nPrice: $[Amount]. Payment due: [on completion / within X days of invoice], by [card / bank transfer / cash].\n\nAccess and supplies: Customer will provide access to [rooms / outdoor tap and power]. Provider will bring [supplies and equipment] unless agreed otherwise.\n\nExisting condition: Before work starts, we will walk through the property together and note any existing damage and any cracked, loose or fragile surfaces. Provider will not clean or wash surfaces listed as excluded.\n\nWeather and rescheduling: Outdoor work may be moved if the weather is unsuitable. Either side may reschedule with [24] hours' notice. Late cancellations may carry a fee of $[Amount].\n\nProblems: If Customer is unhappy with any part of the work, please tell Provider within [48] hours so it can be put right.\n\nPhotos: Customer [does / does not] give permission for before and after photos to be used in Provider's marketing, with no faces, addresses or personal items shown.\n\nSigned: _________________________ (Customer)      Date: _______\nSigned: _________________________ (Provider)      Date: _______\n\nThis is a starting template, not legal advice. Have it checked for your area."}], "videos": [], "tools": {"type": "local", "panels": ["jobs", "estimates", "maintenance", "supplies", "reviews"]}},
 "guides":[
  {"vid": "816cf2db", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "What cleaning and pressure washing jobs really look like", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The two sides of this business, who hires you, and what a normal job actually involves.", "action": "Walk around your block and list five places that need cleaning or washing, and which service each one would need.", "takeaway": "You sell a clean result, a free afternoon and someone people can trust at the door.", "content": "This business has two sides that look alike from the outside but work very differently. Cleaning means going into homes and small offices and leaving them tidy: kitchens, bathrooms, floors, dusting, trash. Pressure washing means going outside with a machine and blasting dirt, mildew and grime off driveways, patios, siding and decks.\n\nCustomers hire you for the same reasons on both sides. They don't have the time, they don't enjoy it, or the job needs gear they don't own. What they're really paying for is a finished result and a free afternoon.\n\nCleaning is mostly indoor, repeat work. The same customer may book you every two weeks. It needs few tools, but it needs a lot of trust, because you're inside people's homes, around their things. Pressure washing is more seasonal and more visible. It needs a machine and more care, since the wrong pressure can damage a surface. It also gives you dramatic before and after photos, which help you find customers.\n\nA normal job goes like this. The customer contacts you. You agree what's included and the price. You show up on time, do the work in the same order every time, check the result with the customer, and get paid. Most of the skill is in the agreeing and the checking, not just the scrubbing.\n\nBe honest with yourself about the hard parts. The work is physical. Weather can cancel outdoor jobs. Some customers are picky, and the first months are often slow while people get to know you.\n\nThe good news is that it's a simple business to start. You need a few tools, a clear offer, good habits and the nerve to ask the people around you for work. This module walks you through setting that up."},
  {"vid": "b8603a9c", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Pick one side to start with", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Why starting with cleaning or pressure washing, not both, keeps your first jobs simpler and cheaper.", "action": "Write one sentence: \"I'll start with [cleaning or pressure washing] because [your reason].\" Keep it where you'll see it.", "takeaway": "Start with one side, get good at it and get paid, then add the other.", "content": "You can offer both sides someday. On day one, pick one. Two sets of tools, two kinds of customers and two ways to get things wrong is a lot for a beginner.\n\nAsk yourself four questions. What can I afford? Cleaning needs a basket of supplies. Pressure washing needs a machine and attachments, though you can rent. Where do I want to work? Cleaning happens indoors in any weather. Washing depends on the weather and on local water rules. What am I comfortable doing? Some people like being inside a home, others prefer being outdoors with a machine. And who do I already know? Friends and neighbors with a dirty driveway or a busy week are your first customers.\n\nChoose one, and give it a few months before you add the other."},
  {"vid": "04f1deff", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Set up your business simply and legally", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The basic steps to name, register and insure a one-person service business, and who to ask about the rules.", "action": "Find your city or county's business license page and write down what it says, plus one question for an insurance agent.", "takeaway": "Check the rules where you live, keep your money separate and get insured before your first job.", "content": "Before your first paid job, set up the basics. None of it is hard, but the rules depend on where you live, so check them locally instead of trusting a general guide, including this one.\n\nStart with a name. Your own name works, or pick a simple business name. Search your area to make sure nobody nearby already uses it.\n\nNext, ask your city, county or state what a business like yours needs. Some places ask you to register the business or get a local business license. Some license pressure washing or home-service work, and others don't. The websites for your city, county and state usually list this, and a call to the local business office works too. Ask what a one-person cleaning and pressure washing business needs.\n\nThen talk to an insurance agent. You'll be working in other people's homes, with strong cleaners and a machine that can damage property. Ask about general liability insurance for cleaning or pressure washing, what it covers if you damage something, whether your personal car or home policy still applies when you use it for work, and what it costs. Customers will sometimes ask if you're insured, so have your answer ready. If you hire helpers later, ask what extra cover or rules apply.\n\nOpen a separate bank account for the business, even a basic one. It keeps income and costs clear, which makes taxes much easier. A qualified accountant or your local tax office can tell you what you owe and when.\n\nWrite each answer down as you find it. You'll use that list again when you set your prices."},
  {"vid": "f5cc607c", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Check water runoff and chemical rules", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Why wash water and cleaning products can fall under local rules, and who to ask before you start.", "action": "Search your city or county name plus \"wash water storm drain rules\", write down what you find, or call the local environmental office.", "takeaway": "Ask your local authority what happens to wash water before you wash anything.", "content": "Wash water doesn't just disappear. It carries dirt, oil, soap and chemicals, and it often runs into a storm drain, a stream or a neighbor's garden. Many places have rules about this, and they differ a lot by country, state and city.\n\nAsk your local environmental or water office what applies to you. Can wash water go into a storm drain? Do I need to collect or filter it for some jobs? Are some cleaning products limited?\n\nWhile you wait for answers, lower the risk. Use the mildest product that does the job. Wet and rinse plants and lawns so soap and chemicals stay off them. Never pour leftover chemicals down a storm drain, and follow the label for disposal. If you're not sure, ask before you wash, not after."},
  {"vid": "dacdc5bc", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Write a first offer with three services or fewer", "time": "3 min", "summary": "How to keep your starting offer narrow, say what's included and turn down jobs that aren't a fit.", "action": "Write your offer: up to three services, the area you'll cover, your working days, and three jobs you'll say no to.", "takeaway": "A narrow offer is easier to price, easier to explain and easier to do well.", "content": "A first offer should fit on one phone screen. When you offer everything, people don't know what to ask for, and you can't price or do any of it well. When you offer three things clearly, people remember them.\n\nHere are a few examples of narrow offers. A standard home clean, a deep clean of kitchen and bathrooms, and a move-out clean. Or a driveway and walkway wash, a patio wash, and a siding wash. Or small-office cleaning after hours. Pick the ones that match your gear, your time and what your neighbors seem to want.\n\nFor each service, write down what's included. A standard clean might mean kitchen, bathrooms, floors, dusting and trash, and might not include inside the oven, inside the fridge or windows. Anything not on the list is an add-on with its own price. This one habit prevents most arguments later.\n\nThen set your limits:\n1. Where you'll travel, for example a 20-minute drive from home\n2. Which days and hours you'll work\n3. How big a job you'll take at first\n\nFinally, decide what you'll refuse. A good beginner list includes mold problems, biohazards, hoarding, roofs, anything that needs a ladder, and surfaces you don't know how to treat safely. Saying \"that's not something I do, but I can help with this\" is a professional answer, not a failure.\n\nYou can widen the offer later, once your first customers are happy. A narrow offer gets you started."},
  {"vid": "821ab4ec", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Do a practice job and time it", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Practice on your own space first, so your first customer gets a smooth job and you learn your real speed.", "action": "Clean your own kitchen and bathroom, or wash your own driveway or patio, with a timer, and note how long each part took.", "takeaway": "Your practice times become the numbers behind your first quote.", "content": "Don't make a paying customer your first test. Do a full practice job at your own home, a relative's place or a friend's driveway, with their permission.\n\nTreat it like the real thing. Write the job down first: which rooms or surfaces, and what's included. Pack your supplies. Set a timer and note the time for each part, such as bathroom, kitchen and floors, or driveway and walkway. Take before and after photos. Notice what slowed you down and what you forgot to bring.\n\nAfterward, ask the owner to look over the result and say what they'd want done differently.\n\nIf a three-bedroom home takes you three hours, that's a real number to use when you quote. Expect to be slower than an experienced cleaner at first, and plan for it."},
  {"vid": "85bc9db7", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Pack a starter kit for home cleaning", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The small set of supplies and tools that handles most homes and small offices, and what to skip at first.", "action": "Make a shopping list of your starter kit and tick off everything you already own before you buy anything new.", "takeaway": "A small, tidy kit beats a trunk full of products you rarely use.", "content": "You don't need a cart full of products to clean well. A small kit covers most homes and small offices, and it all fits in one caddy and one bag.\n\nHere's a sensible starter list:\n1. A bucket or caddy to carry everything\n2. A stack of microfiber cloths in different colors, so you can keep bathroom cloths away from kitchen cloths\n3. A non-scratch scrub sponge, a soft brush and an old toothbrush for corners\n4. An all-purpose cleaner, a glass cleaner and a bathroom cleaner\n5. A toilet brush and a squeegee for glass and shower doors\n6. A broom, a dustpan and a mop with washable pads\n7. A vacuum you can carry up and down stairs\n8. Rubber gloves and trash bags\n\nKeep it simple. Choose ordinary, widely sold products with clear labels, and read each label to see which surfaces it's safe for. Natural stone, wood and some finishes can be damaged by products that work fine on tile.\n\nAsk each customer whether you should use their vacuum and products or bring your own. Many customers like you to bring everything. Others have a preference, such as fragrance-free products or pet-safe cleaners. Ask during your first conversation, not after.\n\nWash your cloths after every job and never carry used cloths from one home to the next. Replace them as they wear out. Buy used or borrow gear where you can.\n\nAdd extras only when a real job shows you need them. A small kit is cheaper, lighter and quicker to pack."},
  {"vid": "611d6228", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Never mix cleaning chemicals", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Some common cleaning products make a poisonous gas when they're combined, so use one at a time.", "action": "Check the label on every product you plan to carry, and write \"never mix\" on your bleach bottle with a marker.", "takeaway": "One product at a time, original labeled bottles only, and plenty of fresh air.", "content": "This is the most important safety rule in cleaning. Bleach mixed with ammonia, which is in some glass and floor cleaners, makes toxic fumes. Bleach mixed with acids, such as vinegar or some toilet bowl and rust cleaners, does too. Never combine cleaners in a bucket or spray one over another.\n\nBuild these habits:\n1. Use one product at a time and rinse between products\n2. Keep everything in its original labeled bottle\n3. Open a window or run a fan, especially in small bathrooms\n4. Wear gloves, and eye protection when you spray\n5. Read the label and the safety data sheet, which the maker usually posts online\n\nIf you feel dizzy or start coughing, leave, get fresh air and call your local poison control or emergency number."},
  {"vid": "f2ea212f", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Choose a pressure washer, nozzles and a surface cleaner", "time": "3 min", "summary": "How pressure and water flow work, electric versus gas, what each nozzle does, and why to rent first.", "action": "Call one local equipment rental shop and ask what it costs to rent a pressure washer and a surface cleaner for a day.", "takeaway": "Rent first, learn what you actually need, then buy the machine that fits your jobs.", "content": "A pressure washer has two numbers that matter. PSI is how hard the water hits. GPM is how much water flows through each minute. High PSI lifts dirt but can also strip paint and wood. Higher GPM helps you rinse and finish faster. A machine with good flow and moderate pressure is often easier to use well than the highest-pressure one.\n\nThere are two main kinds. Electric machines are lighter, quieter and usually cheaper, with less power. They suit patios, small driveways and light work. Gas machines are stronger, louder and heavier, and they need fuel and upkeep. They suit bigger jobs, but they're a lot to learn first. Never run a gas engine in a garage or any closed space, because the exhaust is dangerous.\n\nNozzles control the spray. They're commonly color coded, but check your own machine's manual:\n- Red, zero degrees, is a pinpoint that can cut into surfaces. Beginners should leave it in the box\n- Yellow, 15 degrees, is for tough dirt on hard surfaces\n- Green, 25 degrees, is a good general nozzle\n- White, 40 degrees, is gentle, for delicate surfaces and rinsing\n- Black is for applying soap at low pressure\n\nA surface cleaner is a round attachment with spinning jets that sits on flat ground. It cleans driveways and patios faster and more evenly than a wand, and it avoids the stripes a wand can leave. For many people it's the most useful add-on.\n\nYou'll also need hoses, a way to connect to the customer's outdoor tap and, for electric machines, an outdoor-rated extension cord. Always ask permission to use their water and power.\n\nRent a machine for a day or two before you buy. You'll learn what you like, and renting costs less than the wrong purchase."},
  {"vid": "c5aec392", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Clean any room in the same order every time", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A simple order of work that saves time, stops you re-cleaning and makes every job look consistent.", "action": "Write your room order on an index card and follow it on your next practice clean.", "takeaway": "Top to bottom, back to front, dry before wet.", "content": "A set order makes you faster and more consistent. Use the same one in every room.\n\nWork top to bottom. Dust and wipe high surfaces first, such as shelves, fan blades and light fixtures, so the dust falls onto surfaces you haven't cleaned yet. Work dry before wet. Pick up trash, dust, and sweep or vacuum before you wipe and mop. Work back to front, starting at the far corner and finishing at the door, so you never walk over a clean floor.\n\nSpray surfaces that need soak time, such as toilets and tubs, first, then do other tasks while the cleaner works. Do floors last. Finish with a walk-around from the customer's point of view, checking mirrors, corners and edges in good light."},
  {"vid": "a1abe0fc", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Know what pressure washing can damage and test first", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Which surfaces are easy to harm, how the damage happens, and the hidden-spot test that protects you.", "action": "Walk your street and list the surfaces you wouldn't wash without a test, such as wood, painted siding or soft brick.", "takeaway": "Start gentle and test a hidden spot before you wash any surface.", "content": "Pressure washing is a quick way to ruin something. Most damage comes from too much pressure, a nozzle held too close, or washing a surface that was never meant for it. Know the risky ones before you quote.\n\nWatch out for these:\n1. Wood decks and fences. High pressure raises splinters and leaves a rough, fuzzy surface. Use a wide nozzle, stay back and follow the grain\n2. Soft or old brick and mortar. The spray can wear away the mortar and the face of the brick\n3. Painted surfaces. Old or peeling paint can come off in sheets\n4. Siding. Spraying upward can push water behind it and into the wall, and vinyl or aluminum can crack or dent. Spray downward, from a distance\n5. Windows, screens and light fixtures. Glass can break and seals can leak\n6. Concrete, stone and pavers. A close, narrow nozzle can leave marks, and paver joints can lose their sand\n7. Roofs, outlets and electrical boxes. Don't take roofs as a beginner, and keep water away from electrics\n\nBefore any job, test. Pick a hidden spot, such as behind a planter or under a window. Use your widest nozzle from a few feet back, then move closer a little at a time until the dirt lifts and the surface still looks fine. Use steady, overlapping passes at the same distance to avoid stripes. Let the customer look at the test patch before you continue.\n\nIf a surface looks fragile, tell the customer you won't wash it or that you'll use a gentler method. Writing those exclusions into your estimate protects both of you."},
  {"vid": "a4db51a1", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Protect your eyes, ears and footing on every wash", "time": "1 min", "summary": "The basic safety gear and habits for pressure washing, from goggles to wet-surface slips to ladders.", "action": "Buy or borrow safety glasses, ear protection and non-slip closed-toe shoes before your first wash.", "takeaway": "Protect eyes, ears and feet, keep the spray away from people and stay off ladders.", "content": "Pressure washers are loud and they throw grit as well as water. Wear safety glasses or goggles, ear protection, long pants, gloves and sturdy non-slip shoes. Wet concrete and soap are very slippery, so move slowly and keep hoses and cords out of walkways.\n\nNever point the spray at people or pets, and keep children well back. High-pressure water can injure skin. If it breaks the skin, even with a small cut, get medical help right away.\n\nThe wand pushes back when you squeeze the trigger. On a ladder, that push can throw you off, so stay on the ground. Use an extension wand for higher spots, or don't take the job. Plug electric machines into a shock-protected outlet and read the manual before every new machine."},
  {"vid": "92fe454c", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Tell your neighbors and friends what you offer", "time": "3 min", "summary": "How to spread the word to people you know and to community groups without sounding pushy or spammy.", "action": "Send your offer message to five people you know today, and ask each one to pass it to one person.", "takeaway": "Your first customers usually come from people who already know you.", "content": "Your first customers will most likely come from people who already know you, or who know someone who does. Don't skip this, and don't feel awkward about it. People often want to help, and they may know someone who needs exactly what you offer.\n\nStart with a short message that says what you do, where, and what the next step is. For example: \"Hi [Name], I've started a small cleaning and pressure washing service in [Area]. I'm doing [driveway washes / home cleans] at a starter price. If you or anyone you know could use it, I'd love to help. Can I send you details?\" Keep it friendly. Send it one by one to family, friends, old coworkers, classmates and neighbors instead of making one huge post.\n\nNext, try local online groups. Neighborhood and community groups on Facebook, Nextdoor and similar sites are common places to find local help. Read each group's rules before you post, because many limit advertising or ask you to use a certain thread. Introduce yourself, share a photo or two and answer questions. Don't paste the same post everywhere.\n\nSome people offer a starter price for their first few customers. If you do, say what it is and when it ends, and don't cut your price so far that the job doesn't pay.\n\nReply quickly. People who ask for help often message several businesses and book one who answers well. A friendly reply with a clear next step wins many jobs by itself.\n\nKeep a simple list of everyone you contact and when you followed up. One polite follow-up is enough."},
  {"vid": "2fe7146a", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Take before and after photos, with permission", "time": "1 min", "summary": "How to shoot photos that show your work, and why you ask the customer before you share them.", "action": "Take a before and after of one spot at your own home, from the same place and in daylight.", "takeaway": "Same angle, good light, and always ask before you post.", "content": "Before and after photos are the best advertising this business has, especially for pressure washing. A clean half of a driveway beside a dirty half is hard to ignore.\n\nTake the before photo from one spot, then take the after from exactly the same spot in similar light. Daylight is best. Tidy the edges of the frame so hoses and tools aren't in view.\n\nAlways ask the customer before you take photos and before you share them. Tell them where you'll post them. Keep faces, house numbers, license plates, mail and personal items out of the shot. Many people don't want the inside of their home shown at all, so photograph indoors only with clear permission. Write the permission in your agreement or in a text so you have a record."},
  {"vid": "54300647", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Set up a free Google Business Profile", "time": "3 min", "summary": "How to show up when people nearby search for cleaning or pressure washing, without showing your home address.", "action": "Go to the Google Business Profile site, start a profile for your business and fill in your name, services, area and hours.", "takeaway": "A complete, honest profile helps nearby people find and trust you.", "content": "When someone near you searches for \"pressure washing near me\" or \"house cleaner\", Google often shows a map and a short list of local businesses. A free Google Business Profile is how a small business can appear there. Nobody can promise you a ranking, but without a profile you're not in the running.\n\nSet one up on the Google Business Profile site using a Google account. For a business that travels to customers, Google generally lets you set a service area, such as the towns you cover, instead of showing a street address. That helps if you work from home. Check the current options when you sign up. You may need to verify the business, and the method can differ, so follow the steps Google shows you.\n\nFill it in completely and keep it honest:\n1. Use your real business name, without extra keywords added\n2. Choose the main category that fits, such as house cleaning service or pressure washing service\n3. List your services in plain words, with a short description of each\n4. Add your service area, your hours and a phone number you answer\n5. Upload your best before and after photos, with permission\n\nGoogle has rules about what a profile may contain, and it can suspend profiles that break them. Read the rules and keep your details accurate. Add a new photo or update from time to time so the profile looks alive.\n\nReviews build on this, and the next guide covers asking for them. A profile with a few honest, recent reviews helps people choose you over a stranger."},
  {"vid": "02a1d30c", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Ask for a review right after a good job", "time": "1 min", "summary": "When and how to ask a happy customer for a review, and what not to offer in exchange.", "action": "Write two sentences you'll text after each job, with a link to your review page, and save them in your phone notes.", "takeaway": "Ask happy customers soon after the job, make it easy, and never pay for reviews.", "content": "A review is a customer speaking up for you to a stranger. The best time to ask is right after the work, while the result is fresh. Ask in person first: \"Are you happy with how it turned out? A short review would really help me.\" Then send a text with a direct link so it takes one tap.\n\nYour Google Business Profile has a link you can share for this. Thank people whether or not they leave one.\n\nDon't offer discounts or gifts in exchange for reviews, and never write your own or ask friends to post fake ones. Many review sites ban this. Reply to every review, good or bad, politely and briefly. A calm reply to a bad review shows future customers how you handle problems."},
  {"vid": "99f4f820", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Give a written estimate people can say yes to", "time": "3 min", "summary": "What to put in a clear estimate, how to quote from photos or a short visit, and a simple booking message.", "action": "Fill in the estimate template for your practice job, then write the short message you would send with it.", "takeaway": "A clear, written estimate with the scope and price makes it easy to say yes.", "content": "Most customers want a number before they book. A written estimate gives them that, and it protects you from arguments later. Keep it short and clear.\n\nEvery estimate should say what you'll do, what isn't included, the price, how long it should take, when you can come, and how long the estimate stays valid. Mention where you'll get water and power if it matters, and what the customer should do beforehand, such as moving cars, pets and fragile items.\n\nFor pressure washing, look at the job first if you can, even for ten minutes. Measure the area, check the surface, note stains, cracks and damage that was already there, and find the outdoor tap. If the customer can't meet, ask for a few photos and a rough size, and say the price is an estimate until you've seen it. For cleaning, ask about bedrooms, bathrooms, pets, how dirty it is and any extras, and offer a quick walk-through or video call for bigger homes.\n\nSend the estimate within a day as a text, email or PDF, with a booking message such as: \"Hi [Name], thanks for talking today. Here's your estimate for [service] at [address]: $[price]. To book, reply with a day that suits you and I'll confirm by [time]. Anything not listed is quoted separately.\"\n\nFollow up once after a few days if you hear nothing. After that, let it go. Some people just won't book, and that's normal."},
  {"vid": "88e2f1b5", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Hand out a simple flyer or door hanger", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A low-cost way to reach nearby homes with one clear offer, and the local rules to check first.", "action": "Design a flyer with your offer, area, phone number and one photo in a free tool such as Canva, and print three to test.", "takeaway": "One clear offer, one photo, one phone number, and check the local rules first.", "content": "Flyers and door hangers still work for local services because they reach people right where the job is, and they're cheap. Design one in a free tool such as Canva, then print it at a local print shop.\n\nKeep it simple: your business name, one clear offer, a before and after photo, your service area and one way to reach you, such as a phone number or a QR code. \n\nCheck your local rules first. Some cities, neighborhoods and apartment buildings limit door-to-door advertising, or leaving items on doors or in mailboxes, and some require a permit for soliciting. Ask your city office, and respect \"no soliciting\" signs. A good place to start is the streets where you've already worked, if those customers are happy for you to mention it."},
  {"vid": "951776ae", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Price a home clean by time, size and scope", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A simple way to turn your practice times into a price, with a minimum charge and clearly priced extras.", "action": "Take your practice-job time, add driving and setup, multiply by an hourly figure you chose, and compare the result with local prices.", "takeaway": "Price from your time, costs and scope, then check it against what's normal in your area.", "content": "The price of a clean comes from three things: how long it takes, what it costs you to do it, and what's included. Copying a competitor without checking those can leave you working for almost nothing.\n\nStart with time. Use your practice job. A home with two bathrooms and a kitchen took you a certain number of hours. Add time for packing, driving and a final check, because nobody pays for those unless you build them in. Allow extra for homes that are larger, dirtier or full of clutter.\n\nThen add your costs: supplies, fuel, insurance, your phone, and the tax money you'll set aside. Decide what you need to earn for an hour of work. Then check your local market by calling two or three local cleaners or reading their websites. Prices vary a lot by place, so don't trust a number from a book or a video, including any example here.\n\nThere are three common ways to charge:\n1. By the hour. It's simple, but it rewards slow work\n2. A flat price per visit, based on the home's size and what's included. Customers usually prefer this\n3. By the room or square foot\n\nWhichever you choose, set a minimum charge so a small job still covers your driving and setup. Price a first clean higher if the home is very dirty, with a lower price for regular visits afterward. Price extras separately, such as inside the oven, inside the fridge, windows and laundry.\n\nWrite the price and what it covers on every estimate. When a customer asks for more, you can say \"happy to add that for [price]\" and nobody feels cornered."},
  {"vid": "6975f3d4", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Price a driveway wash by the square foot", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Measure the area, multiply by a rate you've tested, and add a minimum charge for small jobs.", "action": "Measure your own driveway or patio in feet, multiply length by width, and time a practice wash to see your real speed.", "takeaway": "Area times a tested rate, plus a minimum charge and a note on condition.", "content": "For flat surfaces, pricing by area is simple. Measure length times width in feet and add up each section. Then multiply by your rate per square foot.\n\nTo find your rate, time a practice wash. Decide what an hour of your time needs to earn, add your costs, and divide by the square feet you cleaned in that hour. Then ask two or three local washers what's normal near you.\n\nExample only: at 20 cents a square foot, a 600 square foot driveway comes to $120. Your rate will be different.\n\nHeavy stains, moss, oil, slopes and a faraway tap take longer, so charge more for them. Price siding, decks and fences by the job. Always set a minimum charge, so a tiny job still pays for your setup and travel."},
  {"vid": "449bfded", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Turn one-off jobs into regular clients", "time": "3 min", "summary": "How to rebook customers, offer a repeat schedule, and remind people when a wash or clean is due again.", "action": "Write the one sentence you'll say at the end of every job to offer the next visit, and save it in your phone notes.", "takeaway": "Ask for the next booking before you leave, and keep a simple reminder list.", "content": "Finding a new customer takes more effort than keeping one. That's why repeat work matters: it gives you a calmer schedule and less time spent chasing leads. It doesn't happen overnight, and some customers will only ever book once. That's normal.\n\nFor cleaning, repeat work is the natural model. Offer a regular schedule, such as every week, every two weeks or every month. Many cleaners charge a lower price per visit for regular clients, because a home that's cleaned regularly stays easier to clean and you spend less time finding work. Decide what discount, if any, makes sense for you. Keep the same day and time where you can. Customers like a routine.\n\nFor pressure washing, repeat work is seasonal. Driveways, patios, decks and siding get dirty again. Suggest a return visit once a year or in spring, depending on your area and the surface. Don't promise how long a clean will last, because weather and shade decide that.\n\nBuild the habit:\n1. At the end of each job, ask \"Would you like me to put the next one in the calendar?\"\n2. Write down each customer's name, address, what you did and the date\n3. Send a short reminder when it's time, such as \"It's been a year since we washed your deck. Want me to book you in?\"\n4. Thank people, and ask if they know a neighbor who'd like the same\n\nA spreadsheet and a phone calendar are enough at first. Apps like Jobber or Square can help later, when you have enough customers to need them.\n\nWhat keeps customers is reliability. Show up when you said you would, do the agreed work and tell people early if something changes."},
  {"vid": "8fc3d643", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Handle a complaint or damage claim calmly", "time": "1 min", "summary": "What to do when a customer is unhappy or something gets damaged: listen, document, put it right, tell your insurer.", "action": "Write a short reply you can reuse for complaints: thank them, say you'll look into it, and offer a time to come back.", "takeaway": "Listen first, record everything, offer to put it right, and tell your insurer early if damage is serious.", "content": "Sooner or later something will go wrong: a missed spot, a scuff, a stain that wasn't there before. How you respond matters more than the mistake.\n\nStay calm and listen without arguing. Ask to see the problem, take photos, and write down what the customer says. Compare it with your before photos and your written scope. If it's a missed spot or a poor result, offer to come back and fix it. If something is damaged, don't deny it and don't promise more than you can pay. Say you'll look into it and reply by a set time.\n\nIf the damage is serious, call your insurance agent soon, because some policies expect quick notice. What you owe differs by place, so talk to a qualified person if the amount is large."},
  {"vid": "a4cd115d", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Keep simple books and set tax money aside", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A beginner system for tracking money in and out, plus a habit for setting tax money aside and a pro to ask.", "action": "Open a spreadsheet with columns for date, customer, job, amount paid and costs, and enter your last practice job.", "takeaway": "Record every payment and cost, keep receipts, and set money aside for taxes.", "content": "Bookkeeping just means writing down what came in and what went out. Do it weekly and it takes minutes. Skip it for months and it becomes a headache.\n\nStart with a separate bank account used only for the business. Then keep a simple record in a spreadsheet or app. For each job, write the date, the customer, what you did and how much you were paid. Record every cost too: supplies, equipment, fuel, insurance, advertising, printing and phone. Save a photo of every receipt. A mileage log for driving to jobs is worth keeping, and a tax professional can tell you what counts where you live.\n\nTake payments in a way that leaves a record, such as card payments through a tool like Square or Stripe, bank transfer, or cash with a written receipt. Always send an invoice or receipt. Apps like QuickBooks can help once a spreadsheet gets too small.\n\nNow taxes. When you work for yourself, in many places nobody takes tax out of your pay for you. The rules differ by country, state and city, so ask an accountant or your local tax office what you owe, which forms you need and when payments are due. Nobody can name the right share without knowing your situation.\n\nWhat you can do now is build the habit. Each time you're paid, move a share into a separate savings account used only for taxes. Ask your accountant what share is right. Until you know, set aside more than you think you need rather than less.\n\nOnce a month, look at what came in, what went out, and which jobs were worth the time. Those numbers tell you when to raise prices."},
  {"vid": "0cbfc850", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Raise your prices when you stay booked", "time": "1 min", "summary": "How to tell when it's time to charge more, how to tell customers, and how to add services slowly.", "action": "Look at your last five jobs and mark which ones paid well for the time they took and which didn't.", "takeaway": "Raise prices for new customers first, and add a service only when the first one runs smoothly.", "content": "If you're turning people down, or your jobs feel rushed and you're worn out, your prices may be too low. Check your own records: which jobs paid well for the time they took, and which didn't?\n\nRaise prices for new customers first. For regular clients, give a few weeks' notice in a short, kind message: \"From [date], my price for this visit will be $[new price]. Thank you for being my customer.\" Some may leave. That's normal, and it frees time for better-paying work.\n\nWhen you want to grow, add one thing at a time, such as the other side of the business, a new surface or a better machine. If you think about hiring help, talk to an accountant and an insurance agent first, because the rules for helpers differ by place."}
 ]},
{"chapter":{"id": "lawn", "name": "Lawn Care & Landscaping", "icon": "🌿", "color": "#4ADE80", "description": "Mow, trim and tidy lawns for neighbors on a weekly route, then add cleanups, mulch and simple planting.", "group": "local", "breakdown": {"group": "local", "oneLiner": "Keep neighborhood lawns neat on a weekly schedule", "cost": "Low to medium: basic gear and insurance", "difficulty": "Easy to start", "pieces": [{"title": "What it is", "body": "You mow, trim, edge and tidy lawns at customers' homes on a regular schedule, then add cleanups, mulch and simple planting.", "points": ["Weekly mowing is the core service", "Cleanups and mulch fill other seasons", "You travel to each customer's home"]}, {"title": "Who it's for", "body": "People who like working outdoors, show up on time and are fine with physical work in heat, wind and rain.", "points": ["You can keep to a weekly schedule", "You don't mind noise, sweat and dirt", "You like seeing a clear before and after"]}, {"title": "A normal week", "body": "Mow on set days by neighborhood, quote new lawns, answer messages, send invoices and keep your gear in shape.", "points": ["Mow tight routes on set days", "Quote and message new customers", "Invoice, log money and service gear"]}, {"title": "What it costs to start", "body": "A mower, trimmer, blower, safety gear, fuel cans and insurance, plus a way to carry it all. Used gear can lower the cost.", "points": ["Core tools plus safety gear", "Insurance and any local registration", "A truck, van or trailer to carry gear"]}, {"title": "Tools you'll use", "body": "Power equipment, a map tool to measure lawns, your phone for photos and quotes, and a simple way to invoice.", "points": ["Mower, trimmer, blower and edger", "Satellite map, phone camera, calendar", "Google Business Profile and a payment app"]}, {"title": "The hard parts", "body": "Physical work in all weather, slow months in the off-season, plenty of competition, and machines that break at bad times.", "points": ["Rain, heat and slow winter months", "Other mowers often charge low prices", "Safe habits matter on every single lawn"]}]}, "modules": [{"title": "Start here: your first mowing offer", "goal": "Understand the work, set up simply and legally, and write one clear weekly mowing offer with a starting price."}, {"title": "Do the work well: gear and safety", "goal": "Choose and maintain your equipment, work safely, and mow, trim and edge each lawn neatly and in good time."}, {"title": "Find and win your first customers", "goal": "Get your first customers close to home, show your work online, and quote each lawn clearly in writing."}, {"title": "Run it like a business", "goal": "Group customers into tight routes, plan for the seasons, keep the money simple, handle problems and know when to grow."}], "checklists": [{"title": "Lawn Care Startup Checklist", "items": ["Check local rules for business registration and licenses where you live", "Ask an insurance agent about liability cover for lawn work", "Open a separate bank account for business money", "Get a mower, trimmer, blower and edger that run well", "Buy glasses, hearing protection, boots, gloves and approved fuel cans", "Measure three nearby lawns and write a price sheet with a minimum charge", "Write your one-page weekly mowing offer and what is not included", "Set up a Google Business Profile and message five neighbors"]}, {"title": "Before Every Lawn Checklist", "items": ["Check fuel, oil, blade and guards before you leave home", "Walk the lawn and clear rocks, sticks, toys and hoses", "Make sure kids and pets are well away before you start an engine", "Put on glasses, hearing protection, boots and gloves", "Trim first, then mow, edge and blow clippings off hard surfaces", "Take an after photo and close gates behind you", "Clean the deck and strap down gear before you drive off", "Send a done text or invoice and log the job in your records"]}], "templates": [{"title": "Weekly Lawn Service Agreement", "category": "Contract", "body": "This agreement is between [Your Business Name] (\"Provider\") and [Customer Name] (\"Customer\") for lawn care at [Service Address], starting [Start Date].\n\nServices each visit: mowing, string trimming, edging and blowing off hard surfaces. [Add or remove items.]\n\nSchedule: every [week / other week] on or near [Day], from [Start Month] to [End Month]. If weather or another issue moves a visit, Provider will tell Customer by [text / email].\n\nPrice: $[Amount] per visit. Extra work such as [overgrown grass, sticks, leaf removal, hedge trimming, bagging] is quoted and agreed before it starts.\n\nCustomer will keep gates unlocked, keep pets inside during visits, and move hoses, toys and other items off the lawn.\n\nPayment: [after each visit / on the first of each month] by [payment method]. Payments more than [7] days late may pause service.\n\nSkips and cancellation: Customer may skip a visit or cancel with [24 hours / 7 days] notice by [text / email].\n\nDamage: Customer should report any concern within [48] hours. Provider will repair or discuss damage caused by Provider's work. Insurance details: [insurance details, if any].\n\nSigned: _________________________ (Customer)      Date: _______\nSigned: _________________________ (Provider)      Date: _______\n\nThis is a starting template, not legal advice. Have it checked for your area."}, {"title": "Lawn Care Quote", "category": "Estimate", "body": "Quote for: [Customer Name]      Address: [Service Address]\nDate: [Date]      Valid for: [14] days\n\nService: [Weekly / every other week / one-time] lawn care\n\nEach visit includes: mowing, string trimming, edging, and blowing clippings off walks and driveways.\n\nNot included (quoted separately): [overgrown first cut, sticks and branches, pet waste, leaf removal, hedge trimming, bagging clippings].\n\nMeasured lawn area: about [Square Feet] sq ft. Notes: [slopes, gates, trees, beds, anything that changes the time].\n\nPrice: $[Amount] per visit. First catch-up cut, if the grass is overgrown: $[Amount].\n\nSchedule: [Day], [every week / every other week], starting [Start Date]. If rain delays a visit, I will move it to the next dry day and message you by [text / email].\n\nPayment: [after each visit / monthly] by [payment method].\n\nTo accept, reply \"Yes\" to this message or sign below.\n\nAccepted by: _________________________      Date: _______\n\n[Your Name], [Your Business Name], [Phone], [Email]"}], "videos": [], "tools": {"type": "local", "panels": ["jobs", "estimates", "maintenance", "supplies", "reviews"]}},
 "guides":[
  {"vid": "c8ede944", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "What a one-person lawn business really is", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The real work, the real seasons and who pays for it, before you spend a dollar on a mower.", "action": "Walk your street and list five lawns that could use regular care, and write what each one needs: mowing, trimming, edging or a cleanup.", "takeaway": "Lawn care is simple, repeat, outdoor work, and being reliable is what keeps customers.", "content": "A one-person lawn care business means you show up at a customer's home, cut the grass, trim the edges, blow off the hard surfaces and leave the yard looking neat. Then you do it again next week. That repeat visit is the heart of the business. Most customers don't want a one-time favor. They want someone dependable who takes the lawn off their list.\n\nAround mowing, you can add work that uses the same skills and tools: spring and fall cleanups, leaf removal, mulching beds, trimming small hedges and simple planting. Start with mowing alone. Add the rest once a few steady customers trust you.\n\nHere is the honest side of the work. Watch out for these:\n1. It's physical. You're on your feet outdoors, in heat, wind and sometimes rain.\n2. It follows the weather and the seasons. Grass grows fast in warm, wet spells and slows in cold or dry ones, so your busy and quiet times shift through the year.\n3. There is competition. Other crews and local teenagers already mow in most neighborhoods. You win with reliability, clear prices and tidy work, not by being the cheapest.\n4. Machines break. A mower that fails on a busy day costs you time and money.\n5. Rules apply. Many places have rules on registration, insurance and chemicals.\n\nThe good news is that you don't need special training to mow well, and the startup cost can stay small if you begin with the basics. Your customers live close to home, so travel is short. This module shows you how to set up simply and legally, then write your first offer so you can say yes to a customer with confidence."},
  {"vid": "98915a6f", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Start with one clear weekly mowing offer", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A single simple offer is easier to sell, price and deliver than a long menu of services.", "action": "Write your offer in three lines: what you do each visit, which day and how often, and your starting price.", "takeaway": "One clear weekly offer beats a long menu of services you can't yet deliver.", "content": "New businesses often list everything: mowing, hedges, mulch, planting, power washing. A long menu confuses customers and stretches you thin. Start with one offer you can explain in a single breath.\n\nFor example: \"Weekly mowing, trimming, edging and blow-off in [neighborhood], every [Tuesday], for $[price].\" That's it. Customers know what they get, when they get it and what it costs.\n\nPick a small service area, such as a few streets or one neighborhood, so you aren't driving all day. Decide how often you'll mow during the growing season, and what happens when grass slows down or it rains.\n\nAdd extras like cleanups and mulch later, as separate one-time jobs, once your weekly stops run smoothly. A simple offer also makes it easy for a happy customer to tell a neighbor exactly what you do."},
  {"vid": "0d3aa186", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Register, insure and check your local rules", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The paperwork questions to answer before your first paid mow, and who to ask about each one.", "action": "Search your city or state small business website for registration and license rules, and call one insurance agent to ask about lawn care coverage.", "takeaway": "Rules differ by place, so check your local rules and ask a pro before your first paid job.", "content": "Before your first paid mow, spend an hour on the paperwork side. It isn't exciting, but it protects you and your customers. Every country, state and city has its own rules, so treat this lesson as a list of questions to ask, not legal advice.\n\nHere is what to look into:\n1. Business registration. Many places want you to register a business name or a business type, such as sole proprietor or LLC. Your city or state small business website usually explains the options and fees.\n2. Licenses and permits. Some places require a general business license even for small local work. Find out what applies to lawn care where you live.\n3. Insurance. General liability insurance can cover damage you cause to a customer's property or injury to other people, like a stone thrown through a window. Call an insurance agent, explain that you mow lawns on your own, and ask what is available, what it costs and what it doesn't cover. Your home or auto policy may not cover business use, so ask about that too.\n4. Your vehicle. If you tow a trailer or carry equipment for work, ask your auto insurer and check local rules for trailers, lights and plates.\n5. A business bank account. Keep business money apart from personal money from day one. It makes everything later simpler.\n\nKeep copies of everything in writing. If a form or rule confuses you, call the office that issues it, or talk to a local accountant or small business advisor. Many areas also have a free small business center.\n\nYou don't need to finish in one afternoon, but get insurance sorted and your registration questions answered before you charge anyone."},
  {"vid": "2f045e52", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Skip pesticides and fertilizer at first", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Why to stick to mowing and tidy-up work until you know your local rules on chemicals.", "action": "Search your state or local agriculture or environment office for pesticide applicator rules and read what they say about paid lawn work.", "takeaway": "Leave sprays and fertilizer to licensed pros until you've checked the rules where you live.", "content": "Customers will ask, \"Can you also kill the weeds?\" or \"Can you fertilize?\" It sounds like easy extra work. But in many places, applying pesticides, herbicides or even fertilizer for pay requires a license, training or both, and breaking the rules can mean fines and liability. Rules differ by country, state and city, so check yours.\n\nFor now, politely say no. You might say, \"I focus on mowing and cleanup, but I can point you to a licensed company for treatments.\" Knowing who to refer people to makes you look helpful, not limited.\n\nYou can still make lawns look better with the right mowing height, clean edges, leaf removal and mulch. If you later want to offer treatments, learn the training and license rules first, and ask your insurance agent how it changes your coverage."},
  {"vid": "d06a4ecf", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Measure a lawn and set your starting price", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Use a free map tool and a simple time test to turn lawn size into a fair, repeatable price.", "action": "Measure the mowable grass on three nearby homes with a free map tool, estimate the time for each, and write a price for each.", "takeaway": "Price by measured grass and time, add a minimum charge, and check what's normal near you.", "content": "Guessing prices is how new mowers end up working for very little. A simple method keeps your prices fair to you and to the customer.\n\nStep one: measure the grass only. A listed lot size includes the house, driveway and garden beds. Use a free satellite map tool with a measuring feature, or a measuring wheel, to estimate the square feet of lawn you will actually mow. Note slopes, fences, trees and flower beds, because they slow you down.\n\nStep two: estimate your time. Mow your own yard or a friend's once and time it, including trimming, edging and blowing off. Then add loading, driving and unloading. Customers pay for the whole visit, not just the cutting.\n\nStep three: pick the hourly amount you need. Think about what you want to take home per hour after fuel, repairs, insurance and taxes, then multiply by your time estimate. Compare the result with what other mowers charge near you. Ask neighbors and check local listings, since prices vary a lot by area.\n\nStep four: set a minimum charge. Small lawns still cost you driving, loading and setup. Choose a lowest price per visit that makes a stop worth your time. Any number you see in an example is only an example; your costs and your area decide yours.\n\nPut it on a one-page price sheet with small, medium and large lawns, plus extra for steep or overgrown lots. You'll adjust it as you learn how long jobs really take."},
  {"vid": "c73f31b9", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Write down what a weekly visit includes", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A clear included-and-extra list stops most arguments about extras before they start.", "action": "Write a five-line list of what every weekly visit includes and three things that cost extra.", "takeaway": "Say in writing what is included and what costs extra, so surprises never turn into arguments.", "content": "Most customer disagreements come from different ideas of what \"mowing\" means. Decide yours now and write it down.\n\nA typical weekly visit might include mowing, trimming around fences and trees, edging along walks and driveways, and blowing clippings off hard surfaces. Things that often cost extra include grass much taller than normal, picking up sticks and branches, pet waste you have to clear first, bagging clippings, hedge trimming and leaf removal.\n\nAlso write down your gate rule (gate unlocked and any dog inside before you arrive), your rain policy and what happens if you can't get in. Put it on one page you can hand over or text. It makes you look professional, and it gives you something to point to, politely, when a customer asks for more than the price covers."},
  {"vid": "db8e3a84", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "The minimum equipment to start mowing for pay", "time": "3 min", "summary": "What you really need for your first customers, and what can wait until paying work is steady.", "action": "List your equipment in two columns, need now and buy later, and price each item at a local shop or in used listings.", "takeaway": "Start with gear that lets you do a neat, safe job, and add more as paying work grows.", "content": "You can start with less equipment than most people think. The goal is a neat result, not a truck full of machines. Start with this core kit:\n1. A mower. A walk-behind mower suits small and medium lots. Look for one built for frequent use, since a cheap home mower may wear out fast when you cut most days. Riding mowers can wait for big lots.\n2. A string trimmer. It cuts along fences, trees and walls where the mower can't reach.\n3. A blower. It clears clippings off driveways and walks quickly.\n4. An edger. A stick edger, or a trimmer that turns on its side, gives the crisp line along walks that customers notice most.\n5. Safety gear. Glasses, hearing protection, sturdy shoes or boots, long pants and gloves.\n6. Basics. A rake, a broom, bags or a tarp, approved gasoline cans, water and a first-aid kit.\n\nYou also need a way to carry it all. A pickup, a small trailer or a van with room can work, as long as everything is strapped down so it can't shift or fall out. Check your local rules for towing, lights and weight limits.\n\nBattery-powered tools are an option. They are quieter and need no fuel, but think about battery life across a full day, charging, and cost. Gas tools run longer between refuels but need more upkeep. Try both if you can before you buy.\n\nHold off on extras, such as a zero-turn mower or a leaf vacuum, until customers are asking and paying for the work."},
  {"vid": "f2012c7a", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Buy used gear, but test it before you pay", "time": "1 min", "summary": "How to tell a good used mower or trimmer from a tired one in about ten minutes.", "action": "Find two used mowers or trimmers listed near you and write the questions you would ask each seller.", "takeaway": "Used gear saves money if you start it, run it and look it over before you hand over cash.", "content": "Used equipment can be a smart way to start, especially a first mower. A well-kept commercial machine can run for years, but a tired one can cost you a workday.\n\nAsk the seller why they're selling, how old it is and how it was used. Ask to see it start cold, then check:\n- Does it start in a few pulls and run smoothly?\n- Is the deck rusted through or cracked, and is the blade bent or badly worn?\n- Do the wheels roll straight, and does the height lever move?\n- Do the handle, controls and safety guards all work?\n\nIf you can, bring someone who knows small engines. Compare the price with the same model new, and budget for a fresh blade and oil. Walk away if anything feels off."},
  {"vid": "31bfc1fd", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Stay safe around blades, debris and slopes", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The safety habits that protect your eyes, ears, feet and hands on every single lawn.", "action": "Put your safety kit together, with glasses, hearing protection, boots, long pants and gloves, and keep it next to your mower.", "takeaway": "Wear your gear every time, scan before you mow, and stop the engine before you touch the blade.", "content": "Lawn equipment is useful, but it can cause serious injuries. Most can be avoided with habits you build from your first day. This is general advice, so also read your equipment manuals and check your local safety rules.\n\nDress for it. Wear safety glasses or goggles on every job, hearing protection whenever an engine is running, sturdy closed shoes or boots with good grip, long pants and gloves. Blades can throw stones, sticks and bits of metal at high speed, so never mow in sandals.\n\nScan before you start. Walk the lawn and pick up rocks, sticks, toys, hoses, wire and dog toys. Note sprinkler heads, drains, holes, and anything near windows or parked cars that debris could hit. Make sure children and pets are inside or well away.\n\nRespect slopes. On a hillside a mower can tip or slide. Your manual will tell you how to mow slopes with your type of machine, and the advice can differ for walk-behind and riding mowers. Skip any slope that feels unsafe and tell the customer why. Wet grass is slippery, so wait.\n\nNever clear a jam with the blade running. If grass or a stick clogs the mower, stop the engine, wait until the blade has fully stopped, and disconnect the spark plug wire or remove the battery before you reach near it. Never put hands or feet under the deck, and never remove or bypass guards and safety controls.\n\nDrink water, take breaks in hot weather, and stop work if there is lightning nearby."},
  {"vid": "ffa39223", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Handle gasoline the safe way", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Simple rules for storing, carrying and pouring fuel so a small mistake never becomes a fire.", "action": "Check or buy approved fuel cans, label them, and decide where you will store them away from living spaces and heat.", "takeaway": "Let engines cool, use approved cans, and keep fuel away from heat, sparks and living spaces.", "content": "Gasoline vapors catch fire easily, so a few habits matter a lot.\n\nUse only approved gasoline containers, and label them. Never use drink bottles. Let a hot engine cool before you refuel it, because spilled fuel can ignite on a hot surface. Refuel outside on bare ground rather than on a truck bed or trailer, and wipe up any spill.\n\nCarry fuel upright and strapped down, in an open-air part of your vehicle rather than in the passenger area. Store cans in a shed or garage away from the house, heat sources and pilot lights. Check your local rules and your manuals for limits on storage, and tell your insurance agent how you store and carry fuel.\n\nUse fresh fuel, and if you have two-stroke tools, mix the fuel exactly as the manual says."},
  {"vid": "39df07d1", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Mow, trim, edge and blow in a smart order", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A repeatable order of work that saves time and gives every lawn the same neat finish.", "action": "Mow your own or a friend's lawn using the order in this lesson, and time each step.", "takeaway": "Use the same order every visit: scan, trim, mow, edge, blow, then look back at the finished lawn.", "content": "A clear routine makes you faster and makes every lawn look better. Here is an order that works well:\n1. Scan. Walk the lawn, remove debris, close gates behind you and note any problems.\n2. Trim first. String-trim around fences, posts, trees and beds. If you trim first, the mower picks up the clippings you leave.\n3. Mow. Cut in straight, even lines and overlap each pass a little. Change direction from week to week so the grass doesn't lean one way and your wheels don't wear ruts.\n4. Edge. Run the edger or trimmer along walks and driveways for a crisp line.\n5. Blow. Blow clippings off hard surfaces and back onto the lawn or beds, not into the street or onto a neighbor's property.\n6. Look back. Walk the lawn once more, fix missed spots and take an after photo.\n\nMowing height matters. As a general rule, don't cut off more than about a third of the grass height in one mow, because cutting too short stresses the lawn. Grass types and seasons differ, so look up the recommended height for your area, for example from a local garden center or extension service. Keep blades sharp, since dull blades tear the grass and leave a ragged look.\n\nMow when the lawn is dry if you can. Wet grass clumps and clogs the deck. If the grass is very tall, raise the deck and cut twice.\n\nCustomers notice straight lines, clean edges, no clippings on the driveway and no gate left open. Finish every lawn the same way."},
  {"vid": "8d87c055", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Sharpen blades and do a five-minute check", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Small maintenance habits that keep your machines running and your cuts clean.", "action": "Pick one day each week for upkeep and write a short checklist for oil, air filter, blade, deck and tires.", "takeaway": "A few minutes of care each day saves hours of breakdowns later.", "content": "Machines that are looked after last longer and cut better. Build two small habits.\n\nDaily, before you leave home, check oil and fuel, look at the blade, and make sure guards, handles and tires are in good shape. After work, clean grass off the deck and machine, because packed wet grass causes rust and poor cuts.\n\nWeekly, check the air filter and sharpen or swap blades as needed. Sharp blades cut cleanly, while dull ones tear the grass. When you work on a blade, turn the engine off, disconnect the spark plug and keep your hands protected. A blade must be balanced after sharpening, or take it to a shop. Follow your manual for oil changes and service times, and keep a small log of dates. Carry a spare blade, trimmer line and spark plug."},
  {"vid": "0b0cac92", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Win your first five customers on your own street", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Start with people who already see you, ask them directly and make saying yes easy.", "action": "Message or knock on five neighbors' doors this week with your offer, and note each answer in a simple list.", "takeaway": "Start close to home, ask in person, and make your offer simple to say yes to.", "content": "Your first customers will most likely be people who know you or live near you. Close-to-home work also keeps your stops near each other, which saves drive time later. Start where you are.\n\nStep one: tell people you know. Text family, friends, teachers and neighbors: \"I'm starting a weekly lawn care service in [neighborhood]. Do you know anyone who could use a hand with their lawn?\" Many first customers come by word of mouth.\n\nStep two: knock on doors or message neighbors. Pick the streets near you and look for lawns that are overgrown or uneven. Knock at sensible hours and keep it short. For example: \"Hi, I'm [name], I live on [street]. I mow lawns on this street every week for $[price]. Would you like a free quote?\" Accept a \"no thanks\" with a smile, and respect any \"no soliciting\" sign. Some places also have rules about door-to-door selling, so check your local rules.\n\nStep three: make it easy to try. A first-visit price or a single trial mow helps people say yes. Don't cut your price so far that you can't afford the work.\n\nStep four: be dependable from the first mow. Arrive when you said, leave the lawn tidy and send a quick text when you're done. Your first customers become your references.\n\nExpect plenty of no answers and no replies at first. That is normal, and it isn't personal. Keep asking, and keep a list of who you asked and when."},
  {"vid": "15e9c0c7", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Take before and after photos, with permission", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Good photos sell your work for free, but only if the customer says it's okay to share them.", "action": "Take a before and after photo of your next lawn from the same spot, and ask the owner if you may share it.", "takeaway": "Shoot from the same spot, ask first, and leave out people, plates and house numbers.", "content": "Before and after photos are the best proof you have, and they cost nothing. A tidy lawn next to an overgrown one tells the story better than any ad.\n\nStand in the same spot for both shots, in daylight, and hold the phone level. Take the after photo once you've blown off the walks, so the edges look clean.\n\nAlways ask first: \"May I share a photo of your lawn on my page?\" Get the yes by text so you have a record. Leave out people, children, pets, license plates, house numbers and street signs that show exactly where someone lives, even if the customer said yes. If someone says no, respect it and move on.\n\nKeep your favorites in one phone album, ready for your profile, your posts and your door hangers."},
  {"vid": "d5dbdf3d", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Set up a Google Business Profile and Nextdoor page", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Free local listings that put your service in front of neighbors who are already searching for help.", "action": "Create or claim your free Google Business Profile with your service area, phone number and three photos.", "takeaway": "Free local listings with real photos and honest reviews help nearby customers find and trust you.", "content": "When people need lawn help, they often search \"lawn mowing near me\" or ask neighbors online. Two free tools help you show up.\n\nGoogle Business Profile is a free listing that can appear in Google Search and Maps. Create one with your business name and choose a category that fits, such as lawn care service. Because you travel to customers, you can usually set a service area instead of showing a public address, so you don't need to put your home address on display. Check Google's current rules, since they change. Add your phone number, hours, a short plain description, a list of services and your best photos. Google may ask you to verify the profile, so follow the steps it gives you.\n\nNextdoor is where many neighbors ask for recommendations. You can set up a free business page, and happy customers in the same area can recommend you. Read the site's rules for businesses, and be a good neighbor rather than posting the same ad again and again. Local Facebook groups and community boards can help too, but read each group's rules first, since many limit advertising.\n\nKeep your details the same everywhere: the same business name, phone number and service area. Add photos after good jobs, with permission. Ask happy customers for reviews. Reviews take time to build, but a handful of honest ones helps people choose you over a stranger.\n\nNever buy fake reviews or write your own. It breaks the platforms' rules and it damages trust."},
  {"vid": "175fb4fd", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Make a door hanger people keep", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A small, clear flyer on a doorknob can bring calls if it says what you do and what to do next.", "action": "Sketch a door hanger in Canva or on paper with your offer, area, phone number and one photo, and show it to a friend.", "takeaway": "Say what you do, where, and how to reach you in under ten seconds of reading.", "content": "A door hanger is a small flyer that hangs on a doorknob. It works best on streets where you already mow, because neighbors have seen your truck and your work.\n\nKeep it short. Put your business name and what you do at the top, such as \"Weekly lawn mowing in [neighborhood].\" Add one clear photo, your phone or text number, and one next step: \"Text me for a free quote.\"\n\nDesign it with a free tool like Canva, and print a small batch first to see what happens. Check your local rules before you leave flyers, since some places limit them. Mailboxes are often reserved for mail only, so don't stuff flyers inside. Honor any \"no soliciting\" sign. Hang each one so it won't blow away, and pick up any that fall."},
  {"vid": "f379ebec", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Walk the lawn and quote it in writing", "time": "3 min", "summary": "How to meet a customer, size up what you see, and send a clear written quote the same day.", "action": "Practice a quote on a friend's yard: walk it, list what is included, set the price and send it as a short text or email.", "takeaway": "Look at the lawn, ask a few questions, give a clear written price, and follow up once.", "content": "A good quote shows the customer you're careful, and it protects you from underpricing a tricky yard. Meet the customer at the property if you can, even for five minutes. A map is a good start, but a walk catches things a map doesn't show.\n\nAsk a few questions. How often do you want it mowed? Are there pets, gates or locked areas? Is the grass long right now? What bothers you most about the lawn today? Is there anything I should avoid, like new seed or sprinkler lines?\n\nLook for slopes, tight gates, lots of trees, beds, fences, roots and low branches. Each one adds time. Start from your measured lawn size and price sheet, then adjust for the extras you see. If the grass is very overgrown, price a first catch-up cut separately.\n\nThen write it down. A quote should list the address, what is included and not included, the price per visit, how often, when payment is due, and how long the quote is good for. Send it by text or email the same day. The quote template in this chapter gives you the layout.\n\nFollow up once. If you haven't heard back in a couple of days, send a friendly note: \"Hi [name], just checking whether you had any questions about the quote.\" Then let it go.\n\nIt's fine to say no to a job that doesn't pay enough or is too far from your other stops."},
  {"vid": "6951c664", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Ask for a review while the lawn looks great", "time": "1 min", "summary": "The best moment to ask for a review or a referral is right after a job the customer loves.", "action": "Text your happiest customer today with your Google review link, and ask if they know a neighbor who might want the same service.", "takeaway": "Ask happy customers for one review and one referral, once, at the right moment.", "content": "Reviews and referrals are how a one-person service grows without paid ads. Customers rarely offer them unless you ask.\n\nAsk when the lawn looks great, or right after a customer thanks you. Keep it short and easy: \"Thanks, I'm glad you like it! If you have a minute, a quick review would really help me. Here's the link.\" Send the link by text so it takes two taps. Google can give you a direct link to your review page.\n\nAsk once. If they don't reply, leave it alone. Never offer money or free mows in exchange for a positive review, because many platforms ban that. For referrals, ask a simple question: \"Do you know a neighbor who might want the same?\" Then say thank you, in person or with a short note."},
  {"vid": "4224e39b", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Cluster your customers into tight weekly routes", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Why neighbors-first scheduling saves hours, and how to plan your days around grass and weather.", "action": "Mark your customers on a map, group them by area, and give each area a day of the week.", "takeaway": "Stops close together mean less driving, and a simple rain rule keeps your week under control.", "content": "Driving between lawns is unpaid time. Route density means keeping your customers close together, so more of your day is spent cutting and less of it in traffic. Two customers on the same street are worth more to your schedule than two on opposite sides of town.\n\nHow to build tight routes:\n1. Pick a service area. Decide which streets or neighborhoods you'll serve, and politely decline or add a travel fee outside it.\n2. Put your customers on a map. A free map tool with pins works well.\n3. Give each area a day. For example, north streets on Tuesday and east streets on Thursday. Keep the same day each week so customers know when to expect you.\n4. Order the stops so you never backtrack.\n5. Say yes first to new customers who fit an existing day.\n\nNow plan for weather. Grass keeps growing, but heavy rain stops you, and wet grass clogs the deck and tears the lawn. Write a simple rain rule: if a lawn is too wet, you mow on the next dry day, and you text customers that morning or the night before. Keep one catch-up day open, such as Friday, for pushed jobs. Check the forecast each evening.\n\nIn hot spells, start early and take breaks. In dry spells, grass slows, so decide in advance whether you'll mow every other week and how you'll price that.\n\nA paper calendar works to start. Apps like Jobber can later handle reminders and scheduling."},
  {"vid": "1ed8dae7", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Turn quotes into weekly agreements and easy payment", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A short written agreement and a simple way to pay keep customers regular and money coming in on time.", "action": "Fill in the weekly agreement template for your next customer, and choose the payment methods you will accept.", "takeaway": "Put the weekly deal in writing, make paying simple, and ask for payment on a set schedule.", "content": "Once a customer says yes, put the deal in writing. A one-page weekly agreement covers the address, what each visit includes, the price, how often, how and when you're paid, how to skip or cancel, and what happens in bad weather. Use the agreement template in this chapter, and have it checked for your area, since contract rules differ by place.\n\nMake paying easy. Options include card payments through a service like Square or Stripe, bank transfer, a payment app, a check or cash. Many customers like paying automatically after each visit or on a set date each month. Choose one or two methods and say them up front.\n\nSend invoices the same day as the work. If a customer pays late, send one polite reminder. If it continues, pause service as your agreement allows."},
  {"vid": "93589603", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Fill the slow months with cleanups, leaves, mulch and snow", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Seasonal work that keeps you busy when the grass slows, and what to check before you offer it.", "action": "Write down three seasonal services you could offer in your slowest months, with the gear, time and insurance each one would need.", "takeaway": "Plan your off-season before it arrives, and only add seasonal jobs you can do safely.", "content": "Lawn care is seasonal. Grass grows fast in spring, slows in summer heat or dry spells, and stops in cold weather. How long the quiet stretch lasts depends on where you live. Plan for it now so it doesn't catch you off guard.\n\nSeasonal services that use tools you already own:\n1. Spring cleanup. Raking winter debris, cutting back dead growth, edging beds and a first mow.\n2. Fall cleanup and leaf removal. Blowing, raking and hauling leaves. Price by time or yard size, and decide where the leaves will go. Check your local rules, since some places limit dumping or burning, or require special bags.\n3. Mulching. Spreading mulch in beds is physical, repeatable work. Measure the beds, work out how much mulch you need, and price the material and your labor separately.\n4. Simple planting and bed care. Planting annuals or small shrubs, hand-weeding and trimming small hedges. Stick to simple jobs you can do well.\n5. Snow removal, where it snows. Shoveling walks or clearing driveways can fill the winter, but it adds risk: early mornings, slips and falls, and extra equipment. Ask your insurance agent about snow work before you offer it, since coverage can differ.\n\nOffer these first to your weekly customers, because they already trust you. Send a short text a few weeks ahead.\n\nDon't add everything at once. Pick one or two services, do them well, and add more if customers ask. Use the slow months to service your equipment, update your records and plan the next season."},
  {"vid": "0b01fe8c", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Handle damage and complaints calmly", "time": "1 min", "summary": "What to do the moment something goes wrong, so a small problem doesn't cost you the customer.", "action": "Write your own four-step plan for damage and complaints, and save a ready-to-send apology text on your phone.", "takeaway": "Stay calm, take photos, fix what you reasonably can, and call your insurer when it's bigger.", "content": "Sooner or later something will go wrong: a stone chips a window, a sprinkler head gets clipped, a strip of lawn is scalped. How you handle it matters more than the mistake.\n\nIf you damage something, stop, tell the customer right away and take photos. Don't hide it or argue. For small fixes, like a broken sprinkler head, repair or replace it. For bigger damage, call your insurance agent before you promise anything.\n\nIf a customer complains, listen first without interrupting, and thank them for telling you. Ask what they would like to see. Fix real problems quickly. If a request goes beyond your offer, explain kindly what is and isn't included.\n\nWrite down what happened and what you did. Some customers will never be satisfied, and it's fine to part ways politely."},
  {"vid": "486da28b", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Keep simple books and set money aside for taxes", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A basic way to track money in and out, and a habit for putting tax money aside as you go.", "action": "Make a spreadsheet with columns for date, customer, amount received and expenses, and log this week's money in and out.", "takeaway": "Track every dollar in and out, keep receipts, and move a share of each payment aside for taxes.", "content": "Bookkeeping sounds dull, but for a small mowing business it is mostly a list of money in and money out. Keep it simple and do it weekly.\n\nStart with a separate business bank account or card. Use it for all business income and spending. It keeps your records clean and makes tax time easier.\n\nThen keep two lists:\n1. Money in: the date, customer, service, amount and how it was paid.\n2. Money out: the date, what you bought, the amount, and a photo of the receipt. Typical costs are fuel, trimmer line, blades, repairs, insurance, your phone, advertising and equipment.\n\nAlso keep a simple log of business driving. A spreadsheet is fine to start. A tool like QuickBooks can take over later if you want it.\n\nNext, set money aside for taxes. When you work for yourself, you may owe tax on your profit, and in many places nobody takes it out for you. Many people move a share of every payment into a separate savings account as it arrives. The right share and the forms you need depend on where you live and your situation, so ask a qualified accountant or your local tax office. This is not tax advice.\n\nOnce a month, look at your totals: lawns mowed, money received, money spent and hours worked. Divide what you kept by the hours to see your real hourly result. If it's low, raise prices, drop far-away stops or work faster."},
  {"vid": "6a54ba0d", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Hire a helper only when you are ready", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Signs you're ready for help, and the questions to ask before anyone else works for you.", "action": "Write down the three jobs a helper would do, and list what you'd need to check about pay and insurance first.", "takeaway": "Add help only when steady work, solid habits and the right paperwork are in place.", "content": "A helper can double what you finish in a day, but they also add cost and responsibility. Wait until you have more steady work than you can handle, your routes are tight and your quality is reliable.\n\nRules for hiring differ a lot by place. Whether someone counts as an employee or an independent contractor, what you must pay, what insurance or workers' compensation you need, and what training you must give are all set locally. Check your local rules, and talk to an accountant or your insurance agent before the first day.\n\nStart with a few hours. Teach your order of work and your safety rules, provide proper safety gear, and check the first lawns yourself. Pay fairly. Your name is on every lawn they cut."}
 ]},
{"chapter":{"id": "detailing", "name": "Mobile Car Detailing", "icon": "🚗", "color": "#94A3B8", "description": "Clean cars at customers' homes and workplaces with a small kit, a simple menu and careful, honest work.", "group": "local", "breakdown": {"group": "local", "oneLiner": "Make cars look new at the customer's door", "cost": "Low to medium: a few hundred dollars and up", "difficulty": "Easy to start", "pieces": [{"title": "What it is", "body": "You drive to the customer's home or workplace and clean their car where it is parked: wash, vacuum, wipe down, glass, wheels and a wax or sealant.", "points": ["Hand wash and dry the outside", "Vacuum and wipe down the inside", "Clean glass and add wax or sealant"]}, {"title": "Who it's for", "body": "People who like practical, physical work, notice small details and are happy to chat with customers. A car and a few basics are enough to start.", "points": ["You like hands-on, tidy work", "You have a car that can carry supplies", "You are patient with details"]}, {"title": "A normal week", "body": "A few jobs to start, plus time for booking, driving, setup and cleanup. Rain, heat and cold can change your plans.", "points": ["Answer quote requests and book jobs", "Drive, clean one or two cars, pack up", "Post photos and follow up with customers"]}, {"title": "What it costs to start", "body": "A starter kit, a decent vacuum, a plan for water and power, and insurance. Costs vary by place, so price your own list before you buy.", "points": ["Buckets, mitts, microfiber, products", "Vacuum and a water and power plan", "Insurance and any local registration"]}, {"title": "Tools you'll use", "body": "Simple hand tools and a phone. You book, quote and take payment from your phone, and clean with a small, well-chosen kit.", "points": ["Two buckets with grit guards", "Plush microfiber and soft brushes", "Phone for photos, quotes and payments"]}, {"title": "The hard parts", "body": "It is tiring work, the start is slow, and dirty cars take longer than you expect. Water rules and weather can block a day.", "points": ["Water and runoff rules vary by place", "Weather and seasons change your calendar", "Scratch claims and difficult customers"]}]}, "modules": [{"title": "Start here: your first clean car", "goal": "Understand the work, check where you can wash, and set up a simple menu you can offer to your first customer."}, {"title": "Gear, products and a safe process", "goal": "Pick a starter kit, plan water and power, and follow a set order inside and out so you never damage a customer's car."}, {"title": "Find and win customers", "goal": "Get your first customers from neighbors, apartments, workplaces and fleets, show your work with permission, and quote from photos."}, {"title": "Run it like a business", "goal": "Price by size and condition, plan your days, handle complaints, keep simple books and learn advanced services before you sell them."}], "checklists": [{"title": "Mobile Detailing Startup Checklist", "items": ["Check your city and county rules on washing cars and runoff", "Pick a business name and look up how to register where you live", "Ask an insurance agent what coverage a mobile detailer needs", "Open a separate bank account for the business", "Buy a starter kit: buckets with grit guards, mitts, microfiber, vacuum", "Write your three packages with a price and a time for each", "Practice a full detail on your own car and time every step", "Set up your Google Business Profile with photos of your work"]}, {"title": "Job Day Checklist", "items": ["Confirm the date, address and package with the customer", "Check that you have permission, water and power at the spot", "Review the customer's photos and note any extras", "Pack clean towels, mitts, fresh water and all your products", "Photograph the car and any existing damage before you start", "Test new products on a hidden spot, then work in your set order", "Walk around the car with the customer and fix anything missed", "Take payment, ask for a review and offer a repeat plan"]}], "templates": [{"title": "Mobile Detailing Service Agreement", "category": "Contract", "body": "Service Agreement between [Your Business Name] (\"Detailer\") and [Customer Name] (\"Customer\").\n\nDate and time window: [Date], [Time Window]\nLocation: [Address where the car will be cleaned]\nVehicle: [Year, Make, Model, Color]\nPackage: [Express Wash / Interior Clean / Full Detail]\nAdd-ons: [List add-ons]\nPrice: $[Amount], due [before / after] the job\n\nPermission to work on site: The Customer confirms they have the right to allow the vehicle to be cleaned at this location, including use of [water / power] if provided. The Detailer may reschedule if local rules or the property owner do not allow work there.\n\nCondition of the vehicle: Before starting, the Detailer will photograph the vehicle and note any existing damage, stains or wear. Heavy soiling, pet hair or odors not shown in the photos may change the price. The Detailer will discuss this with the Customer before starting.\n\nNot included: [deep stain removal, mold, engine bay, paint correction, other]\n\nPhoto permission: The Detailer may post photos of the vehicle with the plate hidden. Yes / No: ______\n\nCancellation: Please give at least [24] hours' notice. Late cancellations may be charged $[Amount]. If weather stops the job, we will reschedule or offer [interior only].\n\nConcerns: Please tell the Detailer within [48] hours if anything was missed, so it can be put right.\n\nSigned: _______________ (Customer)   Date: _______\nSigned: _______________ (Detailer)   Date: _______\n\nThis is a starting template, not legal advice. Have it checked for your area."}, {"title": "Quote From Photos Message", "category": "Estimate", "body": "Hi [Customer Name],\n\nThanks for getting in touch with [Your Business Name]. Here is your quote for your [Year, Make, Model], based on the photos you sent.\n\nPackage: [Express Wash / Interior Clean / Full Detail]\nVehicle size: [Small / Mid-size / Large]\nBase price: $[Amount]\nCondition extras: [Pet hair / Heavy stains / Other] $[Amount]\nTotal: $[Amount]\n\nIncludes: [List what is included]\nDoes not include: [List what is not included]\n\nWhere and when: [Date], arriving between [Time] and [Time] at [Address].\n\nWhat I need from you: [A shaded spot / access to an outdoor faucet and an outlet / permission to work at this address]. If water or power are not available, I will bring my own.\n\nIf the car is in worse shape than the photos show, I will tell you before I start and we can decide together what to do.\n\nThis quote is valid for [7] days. Reply \"yes\" to book.\n\nThanks,\n[Your Name]\n[Phone] | [Email]"}], "videos": [], "tools": {"type": "local", "panels": ["jobs", "estimates", "maintenance", "supplies", "reviews"]}},
 "guides":[
  {"vid": "246bd927", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "What a mobile detailer actually does all day", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A plain look at the work, the hours, the customers and the parts that are harder than they look.", "action": "Write down the last three times you paid for a car wash or detail, and what you liked and disliked about each one.", "takeaway": "It is steady, physical work that pays for care and skill, not for speed.", "content": "Mobile detailing means you drive to the customer and clean their car right where it is parked, at home or at work. You bring your own supplies and do the job by hand: wash the outside, vacuum and wipe down the inside, clean the glass and wheels, and add a coat of wax or sealant. A light polish can come later, once you have practiced.\n\nPeople pay for this because it saves them time. They skip the line at the car wash, and they get more care than a machine gives. You are selling a clean car and an easier week, not just soap and towels.\n\nA normal job takes a few hours, depending on the car and how dirty it is. Your day is part cleaning, part driving, part talking to customers and part loading and unloading. It is physical work. You will kneel, bend, scrub and carry water, and feel the weather. A rainy week can empty your calendar.\n\nThe hard parts are real:\n1. The start is slow, because nobody knows you yet.\n2. Dirty cars take longer than they look. Pet hair, sand and spilled drinks can double a job.\n3. Water rules and permission can decide where you are allowed to work. The next guide covers this.\n4. The car belongs to someone else, so care matters more than speed.\n\nThe good news is that you can start small. A modest set of supplies, a vehicle that can carry them and some practice can get you to your first paying job. Costs vary by place, so price your own list before you buy anything.\n\nBy the end of this first module you will understand the work, know what to check where you live, and have a simple menu for your first customer."},
  {"vid": "41780cb3", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Wash only where washing is allowed", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Why runoff rules, building rules and permission decide where you can work, and who to ask.", "action": "Search your city or county name plus \"washing vehicles runoff\" and write down what the official page says about washing at home and for business.", "takeaway": "Check your local rules and ask permission before you wet a single car.", "content": "Soapy water that runs into a storm drain often flows to a creek or river without being cleaned. Because of that, many cities and counties limit outdoor car washing, and some add extra rules for people who wash cars as a business. The rules differ by country, state and city, so check your local rules instead of guessing. Your city or county website is the place to start.\n\nThen there is permission. Many apartment buildings, condo associations and workplaces do not allow washing on site, even when the city does. Ask the owner or manager first, and get the answer in writing, even if it is a short email.\n\nIf water is the problem, you can still work. Rinseless and waterless products use very little water, and you will learn them later in this course."},
  {"vid": "f3d0ae5f", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Build a three-package menu you can finish on time", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A short menu of three packages keeps pricing simple and keeps every job inside your skill.", "action": "Write your three packages on one page with a name, a list of what is included, and the time you expect each to take on a mid-size car.", "takeaway": "Three clear packages beat a long menu you cannot deliver.", "content": "Beginners often offer everything. That confuses customers and turns jobs into all-day battles. Start with three packages and nothing else.\n\nHere is one example menu. Change it to fit your area and your skills.\n1. Express Wash. Hand wash and dry, wheels and tires, and outside windows. Good for a car that is already fairly clean.\n2. Interior Clean. Vacuum, wipe down surfaces, and clean the inside glass and door jambs. Good for family cars and messy back seats.\n3. Full Detail. Everything in the first two, plus a coat of wax or sealant and a careful check at the end.\n\nWhy only three? A short menu makes the choice easy, and most customers want to pick quickly and trust you. It also protects you, because you only sell what you can do well with the tools you own.\n\nLeave the advanced work off for now. Ceramic coating and heavy paint correction take real training, and a mistake can damage someone's paint. You will see in module four why it is worth learning properly before you sell it.\n\nTime your packages on your own car or a friend's before you promise anything. Write down how long each one really takes, including setup and packing up. If an Interior Clean takes you two hours, tell customers two hours, not the one hour you hoped for. Almost every beginner is slower than expected, and that is normal.\n\nAdd-ons can come later, such as pet hair removal, headlight cleaning or a clay bar treatment. Keep them on a separate line so the main menu stays easy to read. You will put prices on everything in module four, so for now just get the shape right."},
  {"vid": "9c93e9ef", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Write what each package includes, and what it does not", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Clear package descriptions stop most arguments before they start and set the right expectations.", "action": "Under each package, add one line starting with \"Does not include\" and name at least two things, such as deep stain removal or engine bays.", "takeaway": "Say what is included and what is not, so nobody is surprised.", "content": "Most complaints come from a mismatch: the customer expected one thing and got another. A clear description of each package prevents it. For every package, write a short list of what you do in plain words, such as \"vacuum seats, floors and trunk\" or \"hand wash and dry the whole outside\".\n\nThen write what you do not do. Common examples are deep stain removal, heavy pet hair, mold, odor removal, scratch repair, engine bays and the underside of the car. Say that very dirty cars cost more, and that you will look at photos first. You will learn how to quote from photos in module three.\n\nPut these lines on your price list, in your booking messages and in your agreement. When customers read them up front, a surprise on the day becomes rare."},
  {"vid": "c7348c67", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Set up the basics: name, registration and insurance", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A simple, legal setup: a business name, the right registration and a talk with an insurance agent.", "action": "Look up your city or county business license page, and email one insurance agent to ask what coverage a mobile detailer should have.", "takeaway": "Check your local rules and ask about insurance before your first paid job.", "content": "You can set up a small detailing business quickly, but do it properly. Rules differ by country, state and city, so treat this guide as a list of questions to answer, not as legal advice.\n\nStart with a name. Pick something short that tells people what you do, and check that nobody nearby already uses it. Then look up how small businesses are registered where you live. In the US, many people start as a sole proprietor, which is the simplest setup, and others form an LLC. The right choice depends on your situation, so ask a qualified person if you are unsure. Your state, county or city may also want a business license, and some places have extra rules for mobile businesses. Your local government websites are the best place to look, and a nearby small business development center may offer free advice.\n\nNext, open a separate bank account for the business, even a basic one. It keeps your records clean and makes tax time much easier. You will use it again in module four.\n\nThen talk to an insurance agent. You will be working on cars that belong to other people, so ask about liability coverage, which can help if you damage something or someone gets hurt. Also ask whether damage to a customer's car while it is in your care is covered. Policies differ a lot, so ask what is covered, what is not, what it costs and how to make a claim. Some workplaces and apartment managers will want to see proof of insurance before they let you work.\n\nWrite down every answer in one place. You will come back to it often."},
  {"vid": "28cc3961", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Do a full practice detail on your own car first", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Practice on a car you own to learn your real timing and make your first mistakes where they are cheap.", "action": "Detail your own or a family member's car this week, using your three-package steps, and time every stage on your phone.", "takeaway": "Make your first mistakes on your own car, not a customer's.", "content": "Before a stranger pays you, clean a car that you or your family own. Do the Full Detail from start to finish: wheels, wash, dry, interior, glass and sealant. Use only the tools and products you plan to carry, so you learn what really works for you.\n\nTime each stage on your phone. Those numbers become your real times, and later your prices. Note how long you spend hunting for things in your trunk, too.\n\nTake before and after photos. Look at the results in daylight and from different angles, then list what you missed. Streaky glass and skipped door jambs are common first-time misses.\n\nAsk a friend to judge it honestly, then repeat on a second car if you can. Two practice rounds teach you more than ten videos."},
  {"vid": "b24ff595", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Build a starter kit without overspending", "time": "3 min", "summary": "What to buy first, what can wait, and why middle-priced basics beat both the cheapest and the fanciest.", "action": "Write your kit list from this guide, look up a price for each item at two shops, and mark what you can borrow or buy second-hand.", "takeaway": "Buy a small kit of good basics, then add tools only when a job needs them.", "content": "You do not need a van. A starter kit fits in a car trunk and a few bins. Here is what to buy first:\n1. Two buckets with grit guards. One holds soapy water, one holds rinse water. A grit guard is a plastic grate in the bottom that keeps dirt from being picked up again.\n2. At least two wash mitts, plus a separate soft brush and mitt for wheels.\n3. Microfiber towels in several kinds: plush ones for drying, ordinary ones for wiping, and a few cheap ones for dirty jobs. Wash them apart from other laundry and skip the fabric softener.\n4. A vacuum. A strong shop vacuum with crevice and brush attachments does the job. You will plan how to power it in a later guide.\n5. A pH-balanced car wash soap, a wheel cleaner, an all-purpose interior cleaner, a glass cleaner that is safe for tinted windows, and a leather cleaner and conditioner.\n6. A wax or sealant, plus foam applicator pads.\n7. Small brushes for vents and seams, spray bottles, gloves and eye protection.\n\nSkip these for now: a pressure washer, a foam cannon, a machine polisher and a van. They can be useful later, but they cost more and need practice. Add them once paying customers justify them.\n\nPrices vary a lot by brand and by where you live, so look at a few shops before you decide. A sensible rule is to avoid the very cheapest items, which can wear out fast or do harm, and the fanciest ones you do not need yet. Buy from the middle of the range. Keep every receipt, because these are business expenses you will record later.\n\nKeep everything clean and organized. Customers judge your kit before you start."},
  {"vid": "ccba32c5", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Why the wrong cleaner harms paint, leather and plastic", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Dish soap, household sprays and harsh chemicals can strip, dry or stain. Use car-safe products and test first.", "action": "Read the label on every cleaner you own, set aside anything not made for cars, and test each new product on a hidden spot.", "takeaway": "Use car-safe products, follow the label and test on a hidden spot first.", "content": "A car is made of many materials, and no one cleaner suits them all. Dish soap can strip wax and sealant and dry out rubber. Some household sprays dry and crack leather or cloud plastics. Glass cleaners that contain ammonia can harm window tint. Strong wheel cleaners can stain certain finishes.\n\nUse products made for cars, use each only on the surfaces its label names, and dilute it exactly as the label says. Never mix chemicals, wear gloves, and keep the safety data sheet for anything strong.\n\nThen test. Before using a product on a new car, try it on a small hidden spot, like a lower door edge or under a seat, and wait a minute. If color transfers or the surface changes, stop. A thirty-second test costs far less than a repair."},
  {"vid": "434e1e06", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Wash and dry the outside without scratching the paint", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The order that keeps dirt off clean paint: shade, wheels first, rinse, top to bottom, two buckets, gentle dry.", "action": "Wash your own car using this exact order, and write down the one step where you were tempted to cut a corner.", "takeaway": "Wheels first, top to bottom, two buckets, and a gentle dry.", "content": "Most scratches come from dragging dirt across paint. This order keeps the dirt away from it.\n\n1. Work in shade on a cool car if you can. Hot panels dry soap too fast and leave spots.\n2. Do the wheels first. They are the dirtiest part, and cleaner and brake dust can splash onto clean paint. Use a separate brush and mitt for wheels, and never use them on paint.\n3. Rinse the whole car from the top down to knock off loose dirt before you touch it.\n4. Wash with two buckets. Dip the mitt in soapy water, wash one panel, then rinse the mitt in the rinse bucket against the grit guard before going back to the soap. Work from the roof down and leave the lowest, dirtiest panels for last. If a mitt hits the ground, set it aside and pick up a clean one.\n5. Rinse again from the top so no soap dries on the paint.\n6. Dry with clean, plush microfiber towels or an air blower. Lay the towel flat and pat or glide it in light passes. Do not scrub, and never keep using a towel you dropped.\n7. Clean the glass, then apply wax or sealant by following the product label, usually a thin coat that you buff off.\n\nLight polish is a separate skill. Learn it on your own car first, with gentle products and a small area at a time.\n\nCarry enough clean mitts and towels to swap them out during the day, and wash them between jobs so one customer's grit never touches the next car. Before you leave, check every panel in daylight."},
  {"vid": "23425f33", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Plan your water and power before you drive out", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Carry what you need or confirm it ahead of time: water, power, and a backup if the customer cannot supply them.", "action": "List the water and power a Full Detail needs, then decide what you would carry and what you would ask the customer for.", "takeaway": "Never assume the customer's faucet and outlet are available.", "content": "Do not assume the customer has a working outdoor faucet or an outlet you can use. Ask when they book, and have a backup.\n\nFor water, carry your own in a tank or large containers, or ask to use the customer's faucet with a hose and a fitting that suits it. Rinseless and waterless wash products use a few gallons or less and leave very little runoff, which helps where washing is restricted. Read the label for what each is safe on, and practice on your own car first.\n\nFor power, ask for an outlet near the car, but have battery-powered tools or a portable power station as a backup. Generators are loud, so many neighbors, apartments and workplaces will not want them.\n\nAlways ask permission before using someone's water or power."},
  {"vid": "9252397f", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Clean an interior without damaging leather or screens", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A top-to-bottom interior routine that is safe for leather, screens and plastics, and ends with a final vacuum.", "action": "Clean the inside of one car using this order, and take a before and after photo of the same seat and dashboard.", "takeaway": "Dry brush and vacuum first, wipe gently, and never spray cleaner straight onto screens.", "content": "A clean interior is often what customers notice most. Work in a set order so you never clean the same area twice. Many detailers do the inside first and the outside second, but pick one order and stick to it so nothing gets forgotten.\n\n1. Remove trash and put loose personal items in a bag or tray, then put them back where they were. Never go through the glove box unless the customer asks.\n2. Do a dry pass. Use a soft brush to loosen dust from vents, seams and buttons, then vacuum from the top down: seats, then carpet, then the trunk. Pull out the mats and clean under them.\n3. Wipe hard surfaces. Spray cleaner onto a microfiber cloth, not onto the surface, so liquid does not run into vents and electronics.\n4. For screens and displays, use a soft, clean microfiber with a tiny amount of screen-safe cleaner on the cloth. Check the car's manual if you are unsure.\n5. Leather needs a gentle leather cleaner and a conditioner, applied lightly. Test on a hidden spot first, since dyed or perforated leather can react badly to harsh products.\n6. Fabric seats and carpets need light moisture. Soaking foam can leave a damp smell or a stain. Work on stains gently, and tell the customer that some will not come out.\n7. Finish with the inside glass, using a clean towel, and one last vacuum of the floor.\n\nSome jobs are hard: heavy pet hair, mold, strong smells, vomit or anything that looks like a biohazard. Agree on these before you begin, and charge for the extra time. You can decline a job that feels unsafe or too unpleasant.\n\nPhotograph any damage you find, such as a cracked dashboard, before you start."},
  {"vid": "f6e7d10c", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Finish with sealant, glass and a walk-around", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A thin coat of protection and a slow check in daylight is what makes the job look finished.", "action": "After your next wash, walk around the car with a clean towel and a flashlight, and fix everything you find before calling it done.", "takeaway": "The last ten minutes turn a clean car into a finished job.", "content": "Wax or sealant adds a thin layer of protection and makes paint glossy and easier to clean next time. Read the label. Most products work best on a clean, dry panel, in a thin coat, with the right wait before buffing. More product does not mean more shine. Keep it off unpainted black trim unless the label says it is safe there.\n\nThen do your walk-around. Look at each panel from a low angle in daylight for streaks, missed spots and leftover residue. Check the glass, wheel faces, door jambs, mirrors, and places where water drips after you leave, like mirror housings and door seams.\n\nLast, invite the customer to look. Ask what they think, and fix small things on the spot. A customer who feels heard is the cheapest advertising you have."},
  {"vid": "32630f5b", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Win your first five customers close to home", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Start with people who already trust you, then neighbors and local groups, and ask every customer for a referral.", "action": "Message ten people you know with one short offer and your three packages, and ask each to pass your name to a neighbor or coworker.", "takeaway": "Your first customers come from people who already know and trust you.", "content": "You do not need ads to get started. Your first five customers will most likely come from people who already know you.\n\nStart with friends, family, classmates and coworkers. Send a short, personal message rather than a mass post. Tell them what you do, show your three packages, and give a starting price for your first few customers. An introductory price is fine for a short time, as long as you call it that and it still covers your costs. Ask each person to pass your name to one neighbor.\n\nNext, look at your own street and your friends' streets. Neighbors see you work and can walk over to ask. A simple flyer with your packages and phone number on a community board, or a post in a local group, is enough. Read the group's rules before you post, and do not leave flyers on parked cars where that is not allowed.\n\nCar clubs and enthusiast groups are worth a look. Offer to detail one member's car at a reduced price in exchange for photos and an honest opinion. People in these groups care about quality and will notice shortcuts, so only do what you can do well and be upfront about what you cannot do yet.\n\nKeep a simple list with names, dates, cars and notes. After each job, ask for two things: an honest review and the name of one person who might want a clean car. Ask while the car is still shining. Most people say yes if you ask kindly and make it easy by sending the review link in a message.\n\nExpect the first few weeks to be slow. A handful of happy customers is a good start."},
  {"vid": "61dba272", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Set up your Google Business Profile", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A free listing that helps nearby people find you when they search for a car detailer or mobile wash.", "action": "Create or claim your Google Business Profile, add your service area and three packages, and upload three photos of your work.", "takeaway": "A complete, honest profile helps nearby people find you when they search.", "content": "When someone searches for car detailing near them, Google shows local businesses. A free Google Business Profile can put you on that list. Search for it by name and follow the steps.\n\nFor a mobile business, you can usually list a service area instead of showing your home address. Check Google's current instructions for service-area businesses, since they can change. Choose the category that fits best, write a short plain description, and add your three packages and your hours.\n\nUpload real photos of your work, with the customer's permission and with license plates hidden. Ask happy customers for reviews, and reply politely to every one, good or bad. Never write fake reviews or pay for them. It breaks the platform's rules and can get your listing removed."},
  {"vid": "7541e064", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Pitch apartment managers, employers and local fleets", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Group bookings at one address save travel time, but you need permission and a clear, simple proposal.", "action": "Write a three-sentence pitch and send it to one apartment manager or employer near you, asking if they allow mobile detailing on site.", "takeaway": "One yes from a manager or employer can bring several cars to one parking lot.", "content": "Doing several cars at one address saves driving time and fills your day. That is why apartment complexes, offices and small local fleets are worth a conversation. They are also where permission matters most.\n\nApartment managers and condo boards decide what happens in their parking lots. Many do not allow washing because of water rules, noise, mess or insurance. Ask politely and be ready with answers: you use very little water or a rinseless method, you keep runoff under control, you work quietly, you carry insurance, and you leave the spot clean. Offer a short trial day. Get the answer in writing.\n\nEmployers can be a good fit. An office with a big parking lot might let employees book you on a set day, as a perk. A human resources or office manager is usually the right person to ask. You may be asked for proof of insurance, so have it ready.\n\nLocal fleets, like a small delivery company, a landscaper or a taxi service, have work vehicles that need to look professional. Fleet work can repeat: the same vehicles on the same schedule. Offer a simple price per vehicle and keep your quality the same on every visit. Fleets value reliability. If you cannot show up on time every time, wait until you can.\n\nKeep your pitch short. Say who you are, what you do, how it helps their people, and what you need from them. Then follow up politely once or twice. A no is common, and it is still useful to know."},
  {"vid": "155ac107", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Take before and after photos with permission", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Good photos sell your work, but you need the owner's OK and a few habits to protect their privacy.", "action": "Add a photo permission line to your booking message, and practice taking the same angles in the same light on your own car.", "takeaway": "Ask first, shoot the same angles, and hide plates and personal details.", "content": "Photos are the best advertising you have, because they show the change. But the car and the home belong to someone else, so ask before you post anything.\n\nAdd a line to your booking message or agreement: \"May I post photos of your car? I will hide the license plate and will not show your address.\" Record the answer. If the customer says no, respect it.\n\nTake the same angles before and after: front corner, side, wheel, seat and dashboard. Use the same light and hold the camera at the same height. Keep people, children, house numbers and papers out of the shot, and blur plates. Do not edit photos so much that they stop being true. Your before photos also show the car's condition when you arrived, which protects you later."},
  {"vid": "5fea5e15", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Quote from photos before you drive out", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Ask for a few photos and answers up front so your price fits the car and the customer is not surprised.", "action": "Write the five questions you will ask every new customer, plus a message asking for photos of the outside, front seats, back seats and trunk.", "takeaway": "A quick photo check shows you the real job before you commit your day.", "content": "A quote that ignores the condition of the car causes problems later. A car that sounds fine can have deep pet hair, sand in every seam or a layer of old dirt. Photos let you see the job before you promise a price.\n\nWhen someone asks for a quote, reply quickly with a short list of questions:\n1. What make, model and year is the car, or how big is it?\n2. Which package are you interested in?\n3. Where will the car be, and is there shade and room to work? Is washing allowed there?\n4. Is there an outdoor faucet and an outlet, or should I bring my own?\n5. Is there anything I should know: pets, smoke, stains, spills or damage?\n\nThen ask for four to six photos: each side of the outside, the front seats, the back seats and the trunk. Photos of the worst areas matter most, and most customers do not mind sending them.\n\nLook for heavy dirt, stains, pet hair, worn leather, faded paint and scratches. Use what you see to pick the package and decide whether the condition adds time. Send a written quote with the package, the price, the date and time window, what is included and what is not, and how long the quote stays valid. A text or an email is enough. There is a ready-made quote message in this chapter.\n\nIf the car is worse than the photos showed, tell the customer calmly when you arrive and before you start, and offer a choice: pay the adjusted price, do a smaller package, or reschedule. Never start the work and spring a higher price on them afterward.\n\nSome jobs are not a fit. If you are not comfortable, say so politely and move on."},
  {"vid": "7b6323b8", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Show your work on Instagram", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Short, honest posts of real jobs help locals find you and trust you, and all you need is a phone.", "action": "Post one before and after photo set from a car you cleaned with permission, naming the car type, the package and your service area in the caption.", "takeaway": "Post real work regularly, and tell people how to book.", "content": "You do not need to be a social media expert. A steady feed of real work is enough. Use a business or creator account, and put your service area, a short description and a booking link or phone number in your profile.\n\nPost what you actually do: before and after photos, a short video of a wash, an interior reset, a wheel cleaned up. In each caption, name the car type, the package and where you work, and tell people how to book. Add a few local hashtags and a location tag.\n\nPost a couple of times a week if you can, and reply to every comment and message. Only post a customer's car with their permission. Do not buy followers. A small local audience that books beats a large one that never will."},
  {"vid": "20c1bf12", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Price by vehicle size and condition, with a minimum charge", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A simple price structure: a base price by size, extras for condition, and a minimum so small jobs still pay.", "action": "Time yourself on a practice job, then write a base price for small, medium and large vehicles for each package, plus a minimum charge.", "takeaway": "Price for your time, supplies and travel, not just to match the cheapest person.", "content": "Pricing worries every beginner. Start with a simple structure, then adjust as you learn.\n\nFirst, work out your costs: your time, the supplies you use on each car, fuel and travel, insurance, and the wear on your equipment. Your time matters most. Decide what an hour of your work is worth to you, and use your practice times to see how many hours each package takes.\n\nSecond, price by size. Charge more for bigger vehicles, because they take longer and use more product. Many detailers use three or four groups, such as small car, mid-size sedan or SUV, and large SUV or truck. Example only: an Express Wash might be $40 for a small car and $65 for a large SUV. Check what detailers charge in your area before you choose your own numbers.\n\nThird, price by condition. Keep a short list of extras: heavy pet hair, deep stains, bugs and tar, very dirty wheels, or a car that has not been cleaned in a long time. Explain these when you quote, and use photos to decide.\n\nFourth, set a minimum charge. A short job still needs the drive, the setup and the cleanup, so a very low price on a small job costs you money. Choose a minimum that covers your time getting there and getting set up.\n\nFinally, do not compete only on price. The cheapest option tends to attract the customers who complain the most and stay the shortest time. Be fair, be clear, and be worth what you charge. Review your prices every few months as you learn your real times, and raise them gently when your calendar is full."},
  {"vid": "4c257e18", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Build travel and setup time into every day", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Driving, unloading and packing up take real time. Plan buffers so you stay on schedule and avoid rushing.", "action": "Plan a sample day with two jobs: add drive time, 20 minutes to set up, 20 to pack up and a buffer, and see how many cars truly fit.", "takeaway": "Count the drive, setup and pack-up, not just the cleaning.", "content": "A Full Detail may take three hours of cleaning, but the job takes longer. Add the drive, unloading and setup, packing up, and a buffer in case it runs over. Late arrivals and rushed work are the fastest way to lose a customer.\n\nPlan each job as a block: travel, setup, cleaning, pack-up and a gap of at least fifteen to thirty minutes. Put customers who live near each other on the same day. Give customers an arrival window instead of an exact minute, and text when you are on your way.\n\nCheck the weather the day before and have a rain plan: reschedule, or offer an interior-only package. Put your cancellation and weather policy in your agreement."},
  {"vid": "ae0a7602", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Handle a scratch claim or a complaint calmly", "time": "3 min", "summary": "What to do in the first hour, how to talk to an upset customer, and when to call your insurance.", "action": "Write your own short complaint script with four steps (listen, look, offer a fix, follow up) and save it in your phone notes.", "takeaway": "Stay calm, document everything and talk to your insurer before agreeing to pay for damage.", "content": "Sooner or later a customer will say you scratched their car or missed a spot. How you respond matters more than the problem itself.\n\nStart by staying calm and listening. Let them finish. Thank them for telling you, and do not argue on the spot. Look at the car together in good light, and take photos from several angles.\n\nCompare with your before photos and your notes. Many marks were there already, and a polite look at the arrival photos often settles it. If the mark is new, do not blame the customer, and do not make promises you cannot keep. Say you want to understand what happened and that you will come back to them within a day.\n\nFor a small complaint, like a missed spot, streaks or a dirty door jamb, go back and fix it for free. That small cost often turns an unhappy customer into a loyal one.\n\nDamage is different, such as a scratch or a broken part. Write down the facts. Contact your insurance agent or company before you agree to pay for anything big, because your policy may have rules about when and how to report a claim. A claim usually means telling your insurer what happened, handing over your photos and notes, and letting them handle the assessment and repair. Policies differ, so read yours now, before you need it.\n\nKeep messages in writing where you can, and stay polite even if the customer is not. If it grows into a dispute you cannot settle, a lawyer or your local consumer office can advise you. This guide is not legal advice.\n\nAfterward, ask yourself what you could change so it does not happen again."},
  {"vid": "13625f89", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Offer a maintenance plan so good customers come back", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A repeat clean at a set interval, at a fair price, gives you steadier work and the customer an easy routine.", "action": "Write one maintenance offer, such as a monthly Express Wash or Interior Clean, and send it to your two happiest customers.", "takeaway": "A simple repeat plan turns a one-time clean into steady work.", "content": "Finding a new customer takes far more effort than keeping one. A maintenance plan gives happy customers an easy way to keep their car clean on a schedule, and gives you work you can plan around.\n\nKeep it simple. For example, offer a clean every two or four weeks, or a monthly Interior Clean, at a slightly lower price than a one-off visit. A car that is cleaned regularly is easier to clean, so a lower price can still be fair. Check that it still covers your time and travel.\n\nWrite out the details: how often, which day or time window, how to pause or cancel, and when payment is due. If you cannot fit regular customers into your calendar, do not offer plans you cannot keep."},
  {"vid": "065111d0", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Keep simple books and set money aside for taxes", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A separate account, a simple record of what comes in and goes out, and a habit of saving for tax bills.", "action": "Open a separate business account if you have not yet, and start a simple spreadsheet with columns for date, customer, service, amount paid and expenses.", "takeaway": "Record every payment and expense, and move tax money aside each time you are paid.", "content": "Bookkeeping sounds boring, but it takes only a few minutes if you do it as you go. Good records tell you whether the business is working and make tax time easier.\n\nStart with the basics:\n1. Keep business money apart from personal money. Use a separate bank account for payments and expenses.\n2. Record every payment: date, customer, service and amount. A spreadsheet works. So do tools such as QuickBooks, or an invoicing tool such as Square or Jobber. Pick one and stick with it.\n3. Record every expense: chemicals, towels, fuel, insurance, phone costs and fees. Keep receipts, and photos of receipts are fine.\n4. Spend a few minutes each week updating your records. Once a month, look at what came in and what went out.\n\nNow taxes. In many places a self-employed person is responsible for their own tax and may not have tax taken out of payments automatically. The rules differ by country, state and city, and this course cannot tell you what you owe. Talk to a qualified accountant or your local tax office early, ideally before your first job, and ask: what do I need to register, what costs can I count, when are payments due, and how much should I set aside?\n\nUntil you have that answer, move a portion of every payment into a separate savings account as soon as you are paid. As an example only, some people start with about a quarter and adjust once a professional tells them the right amount. That money is not for spending. It is set aside for the tax bill.\n\nCheck your numbers against your prices every month. If they do not work, change your prices or your packages, not your records."},
  {"vid": "584795a6", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Learn ceramic coating and paint correction before you sell them", "time": "1 min", "summary": "These advanced services pay more but carry real risk, so train and practice before you offer them.", "action": "Look up two hands-on detailing classes or reputable instructors near you, and write down what each teaches and what it costs.", "takeaway": "Learn advanced work properly and practice on test panels before it touches a customer's car.", "content": "Ceramic coatings and paint correction can bring bigger jobs, but they are advanced work. Paint correction uses a machine polisher to remove a thin layer of clear coat, and too much pressure or heat can burn through it. A coating bonds to the paint, so any mistake under it gets sealed in. These services cost more because the skill and risk are higher.\n\nDo not sell either until you can do it safely. Take a hands-on class from a reputable instructor, read the manufacturer's instructions, and practice on spare panels. Learn how to measure paint thickness, because thin paint cannot be polished safely.\n\nTell your insurance agent before you offer advanced services, and be honest with customers about what a coating does and does not do. Do not promise it stops scratches or lasts forever."}
 ]},
{"chapter":{"id": "handyman", "name": "Handyman & Home Repair", "icon": "🛠️", "color": "#F97316", "description": "Do small repairs, installs and assembly around homes, from TV mounts to wall patches, safely and within local rules.", "group": "local", "breakdown": {"group": "local", "oneLiner": "Fix, mount and assemble small things in people's homes", "cost": "Low to medium: tools, insurance and any local fees", "difficulty": "Medium", "pieces": [{"title": "What it is", "body": "You do small repairs and installs in customers' homes, like mounting TVs, assembling furniture, patching walls and fixing doors.", "points": ["Mount TVs, shelves and mirrors", "Patch, paint and caulk small areas", "Fix doors and swap hardware"]}, {"title": "Who it's for", "body": "People who are handy, tidy and dependable, and who are happy talking with customers. You also need to be fine with physical work.", "points": ["Reliable and on time", "Comfortable on ladders and on your feet", "Patient with customers and small jobs"]}, {"title": "A normal week", "body": "You answer inquiries, quote a few jobs, batch the work by area, then invoice and update your books. Weeks can be uneven, especially at the start.", "points": ["Reply to inquiries and book visits", "Batch jobs by area and by day", "Invoice, photos and simple books"]}, {"title": "What it costs to start", "body": "Mostly tools, safety gear, a stepladder and insurance, plus any registration or license fees. Costs vary a lot by area, so get local quotes.", "points": ["Starter tools and safety gear", "Liability insurance and registration", "A vehicle, fuel and a business phone"]}, {"title": "Tools you'll use", "body": "A cordless drill, level, stud finder and hand tools do most of the work. Phone apps handle photos, scheduling, invoices and payments.", "points": ["Drill, level, stud finder, stepladder", "Square or Stripe to take payments", "Google Business Profile and a calendar"]}, {"title": "The hard parts", "body": "Your limits come first: licensed trades, dollar caps and safety. Starts can be slow, the work is physical, and small jobs mean thin margins.", "points": ["Staying inside local license rules", "A slow start and uneven weeks", "Physical work and thin margins"]}]}, "modules": [{"title": "Start here: a small, safe job list", "goal": "Choose five jobs you can do well, learn where handyman work stops, register simply and write your first job menu."}, {"title": "Do the work well and safely", "goal": "Build a starter tool kit, work safely, agree the scope in writing and photograph every job."}, {"title": "Find and win customers", "goal": "Get your first jobs from people you know, neighbors, landlords and Google, and turn each finished job into the next."}, {"title": "Run it like a business", "goal": "Price jobs, handle problems calmly, batch your schedule, keep simple books and win repeat work."}], "checklists": [{"title": "Handyman Startup Checklist", "items": ["Write your list of five jobs you can do well", "Look up your local rules on license limits and dollar caps", "Register your business and ask about a city business license", "Open a separate business bank account", "Get insurance quotes and buy cover before your first paid job", "Gather a starter tool kit, safety gear and a stepladder", "Write your job menu and send it to three people you know", "Set up your Google Business Profile"]}, {"title": "Before-Every-Job Checklist", "items": ["Confirm the job is on your list and needs no licensed trade", "Agree the scope, price and materials in writing", "Take before photos, with the customer's OK", "Find the power panel and water shutoff and check behind the wall", "Pack tools, materials, safety gear and drop cloths", "Confirm your arrival window the day before", "Clean up, walk the customer through the work and take after photos", "Send the invoice and ask for a review"]}], "templates": [{"title": "Handyman Job Agreement", "category": "Contract", "body": "Job Agreement\n\nThis agreement is between [Your Business Name] (\"Handyman\") and [Customer Name] (\"Customer\") for work at [Job Address] on or about [Date].\n\nWork to be done: [List each task in plain words, for example: mount one TV on the living room wall and cover the cable].\n\nNot included: [List anything not covered, for example: painting after patching, haul-away]. The Handyman does not do electrical, plumbing, gas, roofing or other work that needs a licensed trade.\n\nPrice: $[Amount] [fixed price / estimated at $[Rate] per hour]. Minimum charge for the visit: $[Amount].\n\nMaterials: [Customer / Handyman] will supply materials. If the Handyman buys them, the Customer pays the receipt cost plus [handling fee].\n\nChanges: If extra work is needed, the Handyman will give the extra price and time in writing, and will start it only after the Customer agrees in writing (a text or email is fine).\n\nAccess: The Customer will give access to the work area and [parking / cleared furniture].\n\nPayment: Due [on completion / within [7] days of the invoice] by [cash, check or card].\n\nWorkmanship: The Handyman will fix problems caused by the Handyman's workmanship if reported within [30] days. This does not cover materials, existing damage or misuse.\n\nCancellation: Please give [24] hours' notice. [Cancellation fee, if any].\n\nSigned: _________________________ (Customer)   Date: _______\nSigned: _________________________ (Handyman)   Date: _______\n\nThis is a starting template, not legal advice. Have it checked for your area."}, {"title": "Handyman Job Estimate", "category": "Estimate", "body": "Estimate from [Your Business Name]\n\nCustomer: [Customer Name]      Phone or email: [Contact]\nJob address: [Address]      Date: [Date]\n\nJob: [Describe the work in plain words]\n\nIncludes: [Tasks covered, tools, clean-up]\nDoes not include: [Anything billed separately, for example: painting, wall repairs, haul-away]\n\nLabor: [fixed price $[Amount] / about [X] hours at $[Rate] per hour]\nMaterials: [Customer supplies / Handyman buys at receipt cost plus [handling fee]], about $[Amount]\nMinimum charge for the visit: $[Amount]\nEstimated total: $[Amount]\n\nThis is a [fixed quote / good-faith estimate]. If the job needs more work than described, I will tell you the extra price and time before I continue.\n\nWork that needs a licensed electrician, plumber or other licensed trade is not part of this estimate.\n\nProposed date and time window: [Date and window]\nThis estimate is valid for [14] days.\n\nTo accept, reply \"Accepted\" or sign below.\n\nSigned: _________________________   Date: _______"}], "videos": [], "tools": {"type": "local", "panels": ["jobs", "estimates", "maintenance", "supplies", "reviews"]}},
 "guides":[
  {"vid": "2cd6e394", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "What a handyman business really looks like", "time": "3 min", "summary": "What the work is, who pays for it, and the honest hard parts before you spend a dollar.", "action": "Write down the last five small repair or install jobs you did or helped with, and how long each one took.", "takeaway": "A handyman business is many small jobs done reliably, safely and tidily.", "content": "A handyman business is you, a vehicle full of tools, and a list of small jobs that people don't have the time, the tools or the confidence to do themselves. Think of mounting a TV, hanging shelves, assembling furniture, patching a hole in drywall, fixing a sticking door or re-caulking a tub. Each job is small. Customers pay for you showing up on time, doing it properly and leaving the place clean.\n\nYour customers are busy homeowners, renters, older people who shouldn't be on a ladder, and landlords who need a unit fixed between tenants. Most have a list of little things that has sat there for months. Your job is to clear it.\n\nKnow what the work is really like before you start:\n- It is physical. You will lift, kneel, climb and breathe dust.\n- Jobs are small, so travel, set-up and clean-up take a big share of your time.\n- The start is usually slow, because nobody knows you yet, and some weeks will be quiet.\n- Demand moves with the seasons and with moving days.\n- Some customers will push on price or ask for more than they agreed to.\n\nThe good news is that you can start with tools you may already own, begin on evenings and weekends, and build a name by being dependable. Reliable is rarer than skilled.\n\nOne rule runs through the whole course. The only work worth taking is work you can do well, safely and legally. What a handyman may do differs by country, state and city, so you will check your local rules soon.\n\nThe plan from here: pick a short list of jobs, learn which work to turn down, set up legally and simply, then do the work well, find customers and run it like a business."},
  {"vid": "211d76b1", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Start with five jobs you can do well", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Why a short job list is easier to price, easier to sell and safer than saying yes to everything.", "action": "Write your list of five jobs, then mark any you have not done at least twice and plan a practice run at your place or a friend's.", "takeaway": "A short list of jobs you do well beats a long list you do slowly.", "content": "New handymen often say yes to everything, then end up on jobs they are slow at or nervous about. A narrow list is easier to price, explain and do safely. Start with about five jobs.\n\nPick them with three questions. Have you done this job more than once? Can you do it with a small set of tools you can afford? Is it normal handyman work where you live, and not something that needs a licensed trade?\n\nGood first candidates for many people are mounting TVs and shelves, assembling furniture, patching small drywall holes, touch-up painting, and swapping door handles and hardware.\n\nPractice any job you are unsure of at your own place or a friend's before you charge anyone. Add new jobs later, one at a time."},
  {"vid": "0e7e75a0", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Know where handyman work stops", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Why licensed trades and dollar limits exist, and how to find the rules that apply where you live.", "action": "Find your local rules page for handyman or contractor work and write down any dollar limit and the list of licensed trades.", "takeaway": "Check your local rules first, and treat anything unclear as off limits until you know.", "content": "Handyman work has a boundary, and where it sits depends on where you live. In many places, electrical, plumbing, gas, roofing and structural work can only be done by a licensed tradesperson. Many places also cap the dollar size of a job a handyman may take without a contractor license, and some ask handymen to register or hold a license even below that cap. In other places the rules are lighter. You cannot guess which applies to you, and \"my neighbor does it\" is not a defense.\n\nDoing licensed work without the license can bring fines, may leave you without insurance cover, and can make you personally responsible if something goes wrong. A wiring fault or a hidden water leak can cause serious damage or hurt someone. No single job is worth that risk.\n\nSo check before you advertise anything:\n1. Search for your state or city plus \"handyman license\" and read the official government page, not a blog.\n2. Find the contractor licensing board and call or email them if the page is unclear.\n3. Ask what counts as licensed work, whether there is a dollar cap per job, and whether the cap includes materials.\n4. Ask whether your city also needs a business license or a permit for the work on your list.\n5. Write down what they say, with the date and who told you.\n\nPermits are a related question. Moving walls, changing plumbing or wiring and similar work often needs one, and that is not handyman territory.\n\nRules change, so look again once a year. If a job is unclear, treat it as outside your limit until an official source tells you otherwise. When the answer is \"that needs a licensed trade\", you pass the job on, and the next lesson shows you how."},
  {"vid": "a6115ab4", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Say no to a job and send it on", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A kind way to decline work outside your limits that keeps the customer's trust.", "action": "Write your own \"no, but here is what I can do\" message and save it on your phone, then ask two licensed tradespeople if you can refer customers to them.", "takeaway": "A kind no, with a name to call, protects you and keeps the customer.", "content": "Saying no is part of the trade, and done well it builds trust. When a request falls outside your list, your limits or your local rules, say so kindly and give a next step.\n\nTry something like this: \"Thanks for thinking of me. That job needs a licensed electrician, so I can't take it on. I can patch and paint the wall afterward. Would you like a name to call?\"\n\nKeep a short list of two or three licensed people you would happily recommend. If a tradesperson ever pays you for a referral, tell the customer. A good list also sends work back to you, because trades are often asked for small jobs they don't want.\n\nNever take a job you should refuse because the money looks good."},
  {"vid": "a8ef5c98", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Register your business and get insured", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The basic setup questions to ask before your first paid job: registration, a bank account and insurance.", "action": "Call one insurance agent and ask what general liability cover for a handyman costs and covers where you live. Write down the answer.", "takeaway": "Register, open a business account and get insured before your first paid job.", "content": "Before your first paid job, set up the basics. The details depend on where you live, so treat this lesson as a list of questions to ask, not a list of answers. Rules differ by country, state and city.\n\nRegistration. In many places you can work as a sole proprietor under your own name. If you want a business name, you may need to register it. Some cities or counties want a business license, and some places want a handyman license or registration. Start with your state or city small business website. A local small business office can often explain the steps for free.\n\nA separate bank account. Open a basic account just for the business. Customer payments go in and business costs come out. It keeps your records clean and makes tax time far less painful.\n\nInsurance. Ask a local insurance agent about general liability insurance for handyman work. It is designed to help pay if you damage a customer's property or someone is hurt because of your work. Ask what it covers and excludes, whether it covers every job on your list, and how to get a certificate of insurance. Property managers often ask for one before they hire you. Your tools, vehicle and health may need separate cover, and personal policies may not cover business use.\n\nTaxes. Ask an accountant or your tax authority what to register for and what records to keep. Do not guess.\n\nAll of this usually takes a few phone calls and some forms. Do it before the first paid job, not after the first problem."},
  {"vid": "7a67d854", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Write a job menu a neighbor can say yes to", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A one-screen list of what you do, what is included and what you do not take on.", "action": "Write your job menu, including a line on what you don't do, and send it to three people you know.", "takeaway": "A clear menu tells customers what to ask for and what you will not do.", "content": "A job menu is a short list of what you do, so customers know what to ask for. It should fit on one phone screen.\n\nList your five jobs in plain words, such as \"Mount a TV\", \"Hang shelves or a mirror\", \"Assemble furniture\", \"Patch a small wall hole\" and \"Fix a sticking door\". Next to each, note what is included. Add a line about your minimum charge for any visit. You will set real numbers in the pricing lessons, and prices vary by area, so check what handymen near you charge.\n\nThen add where you work and what you don't do, such as \"No electrical, plumbing or gas work.\" End with how to reach you.\n\nKeep it honest and short. Send it to three people and ask which job they would book first."},
  {"vid": "1558cc68", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Build a starter tool kit in stages", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The tools that cover most small jobs, where quality matters, and how to add the rest as work comes in.", "action": "List the tools you already own, mark what is missing for your five jobs, and circle the one purchase that would unlock the most work.", "takeaway": "Buy what your first five jobs need, spend on safety and daily-use tools, and add the rest as jobs pay.", "content": "You do not need a full workshop to start. You need a kit that handles your five jobs, and you can build it job by job. Let early jobs pay for later purchases.\n\nA sensible starter kit for common small jobs:\n- Cordless drill and driver with drill bits and driver bits\n- Tape measure, level, pencil and stud finder\n- Hammer, screwdrivers, pliers, adjustable wrench and utility knife\n- Putty knives, sanding block, caulk gun and a few paint brushes\n- An assortment of screws, wall anchors and other fasteners\n- A stepladder that suits the height you work at\n- Safety glasses, gloves, a dust mask or respirator, and drop cloths\n- A work light, a small broom or vacuum, and a toolbox\n\nQuality matters, but not everywhere. Spend more on what you hold all day or what protects you: the drill, the ladder, the level, and your eye and breathing protection. Mid-range is fine for tools you use now and then. The very cheapest tape measures and bits wear out fast and cost you time.\n\nPick one cordless battery system and stay with it, so batteries work across your tools. Replace worn bits and blades before they ruin a job. Keep your receipts, because tools you use for work may count as a business cost, and an accountant can tell you how to record them.\n\nPack your kit so it is ready to go. A small bag for each kind of job saves trips back to the vehicle, and a minute at the end of each day to restock screws and anchors saves a lot of hassle."},
  {"vid": "2386ced3", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Rent or borrow the tool you will use once", "time": "1 min", "summary": "How to avoid tying up cash in expensive tools you will rarely touch.", "action": "Pick one tool you want but don't own, look up its rental price, and decide whether renting makes more sense.", "takeaway": "Rent or borrow tools you rarely use, and buy only when a booked job will pay for them.", "content": "Some tools cost a lot and get used once or twice a year. A tile saw, a pressure washer and a tall extension ladder are examples. Buying them early ties up cash and fills your garage.\n\nAsk first: will I use this a few times in the next few months? If not, rent it from a home center or tool rental shop, or borrow it from a friend who knows how to use it. Check the rental price before you quote, and include it in your estimate.\n\nIf you do buy, buy for a job you have already booked and let that job pay for it.\n\nUnfamiliar tools can be dangerous. Ask the staff to show you how the tool works and what safety gear it needs."},
  {"vid": "021836db", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Work safely: ladders, dust and your eyes", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The habits that keep you in one piece on ladders, around dust and with loud or sharp tools.", "action": "Check your stepladder for damage and its weight rating, and put safety glasses, a dust mask and earplugs where you will grab them first.", "takeaway": "Most handyman injuries are preventable, so slow down and wear the gear every time.", "content": "You are your own safety department, and you cannot work if you are hurt. A few habits cover most of the risk.\n\nLadders. Pick one tall enough that you never stand on the top step or lean far to the side. Set it on firm, level ground, fully open and locked. Keep your belt buckle between the rails and move the ladder instead of reaching. Check the ladder's weight rating against you plus your tools. Keep three points of contact when you climb, and do not carry loads that unbalance you. For anything high, steep or awkward, bring a second person or leave the job.\n\nDust. Sanding drywall and cutting materials make fine dust you should not breathe. Wear a properly fitted mask or respirator, close doors, cover floors and furniture, and use a vacuum rather than a dry broom. Older homes can contain lead paint or asbestos. If you suspect either, stop. Do not sand, scrape or cut it. Ask your local health or environmental agency, or a testing professional, what the rules and options are where you live.\n\nEyes and ears. Wear safety glasses whenever you drill, cut, sand or hammer. Use hearing protection with loud tools.\n\nBody. Lift with your legs, ask for help with heavy items such as large TVs and cabinets, and take breaks. Keep a small first-aid kit in your vehicle.\n\nWorking alone makes all of this matter more. Tell someone where you are working, and keep your phone on you."},
  {"vid": "fe5fbbe4", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Turn off the power and water, and never touch live wires", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Where the shutoffs are, and why live electrics and surprise pipes mean stop and call a licensed trade.", "action": "At your own home, find the breaker panel and the main water shutoff, and label them so anyone could find them in a hurry.", "takeaway": "Find the shutoffs before you start, and if you are unsure whether power is off, stop.", "content": "Before any job near wiring or plumbing, find the breaker panel and the water shutoff. Ask the customer to show you.\n\nNever work on live electrics. In many places even simple electrical work, such as swapping a light fixture, is reserved for licensed electricians, so check your local rules before you offer it. Where it is allowed, switch the circuit off at the breaker, then use a non-contact voltage tester to confirm the wires are dead. Test the tester first. If you cannot be sure the power is off, stop.\n\nFor water, close the valve before you touch a faucet or fitting, then open a tap to release pressure. Keep towels nearby.\n\nIf you hit a pipe or a wire, stop, shut off the supply and call a licensed trade."},
  {"vid": "2edcd348", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Write the scope and agreement before the job", "time": "3 min", "summary": "What to put on one page so both sides know the work, the price, who buys materials and what happens if the job grows.", "action": "Fill in the job agreement template in this chapter for a pretend job, then read it as the customer and fix anything unclear.", "takeaway": "If it is not written down, you will end up arguing about it later.", "content": "Most problems between handymen and customers come from different ideas about what was agreed. A one-page written scope fixes this. It also shows the customer you are organized.\n\nCover these points in plain words:\n1. The work. List each task, for example \"mount one TV on the living room wall and cover the cable\".\n2. What is not included. Say \"no electrical or plumbing work\" and name anything else that people often assume, like painting after a patch.\n3. The price, or how it is worked out, plus your minimum charge.\n4. Materials. Say who buys them and how you will charge for them.\n5. Timing and access. Give the date, the arrival window and what the customer needs to clear or provide.\n6. Payment. Say when it is due and how you accept it.\n7. Changes. Say that extra work is priced and agreed in writing before you start it.\n\nThe changes point matters most. Jobs grow. You open a wall and find rot, or the customer adds \"just one more thing\". Write a short change note: what you found, the extra work, the extra price and time, and ask for a yes by text or email. Then continue.\n\nKeep it short enough that people actually read it. A signature is best, and a clear \"yes\" by email or text is far better than nothing. Keep a copy.\n\nContract rules differ by place, and some areas add wording or cancellation rights for home work above a certain size. Check your local rules, and have a template reviewed by a qualified person for your area."},
  {"vid": "b9013b43", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Photograph every job before, during and after", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Simple photos that protect you from disputes, help you sell, and respect the customer's privacy.", "action": "On your next job, take a wide shot and a close-up before you start, and the same two after you finish, in one folder per customer.", "takeaway": "Photos cost nothing, settle arguments and sell your work.", "content": "Photos protect you and help you win work. Take them on every job.\n\nBefore you start, photograph the work area from a wide angle and up close. Include any existing scratches, cracks or damage, so nobody blames you later. During the job, photograph anything that will be hidden when you close up, such as what was inside a wall or where you found studs. After you finish, take a wide shot and a close-up of the clean result.\n\nSave them in a folder named for the customer and date, and back them up.\n\nAsk permission before you use any photo for your profile or social posts. Leave out faces, house numbers, mail, family photos and valuables. A photo of a neat, finished job is the best advertising you can have."},
  {"vid": "2143e49a", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Win your first jobs from people you already know", "time": "3 min", "summary": "How to turn friends, family, coworkers and neighbors into your first paying customers without being pushy.", "action": "Write a list of 30 people you know, then send a personal message to ten of them with your job menu.", "takeaway": "Your first customers are usually people who already know and trust you.", "content": "Your first jobs will almost always come from people who know you: family, friends, coworkers, classmates, neighbors and people in your building. They already trust you, which is exactly what a stranger needs time to build.\n\nStart by writing a list of 30 names. Do not judge it. Then send a short, personal message to each person, not a group blast. Say what you do, say you are taking on small jobs, attach your job menu and ask if they have a list of little things that needs doing. A simple version: \"Hi Sam, I've started a small handyman business doing TV mounting, shelves, furniture assembly and wall patching. If you have a short list of odd jobs, I'd be glad to help. Here's what I offer.\"\n\nThen ask one more thing: \"Do you know anyone else who has a to-do list?\" Referrals from people who trust you often carry more weight than any ad.\n\nA few cautions:\n- Treat friends like any customer. Agree the scope and price in writing, because unclear jobs strain friendships.\n- Do not work for free. If you offer a first-job discount, make it small, say it is a one-time offer and name the full price.\n- Do the first jobs especially well, take photos with permission, and ask for a review.\n\nExpect a slow start. Some people will not reply, and some will say later. Follow up politely once after a week or two. Your first five jobs are as much about learning and proof as about earning."},
  {"vid": "6c7b74e2", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Write a neighborhood post that gets replies", "time": "1 min", "summary": "What to put in a Nextdoor or local group post so people see a real, reliable local handyman.", "action": "Read the rules of one neighborhood group, then draft a short post with your menu, your area and one photo of finished work.", "takeaway": "A friendly, specific, honest post beats a loud one, and you should post once.", "content": "Neighborhood apps such as Nextdoor and local community groups can bring in your first local customers. First read the group's rules. Some limit business posts or have a section for them. Follow the rules.\n\nA good post is short and specific: who you are, the town or area you cover, the five or so jobs you do, one photo of finished work with the owner's permission, and how to reach you. Add a line saying what you do not do, such as electrical or plumbing work.\n\nDo not post every day or copy it across many groups. Post once, and answer every reply quickly and politely. If neighbors ask for recommendations for other trades, help them. Being useful in a group does more for your name than any advert."},
  {"vid": "6b60b9a3", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Land steady work from landlords and property managers", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Why landlords and property managers hire handymen repeatedly, and how to approach them professionally.", "action": "Find three local property managers, landlords or rental agencies and send each one a short email with your job menu.", "takeaway": "Property managers want fast, tidy, reliable help, and one good account can bring steady small jobs.", "content": "Landlords and property managers have small repair jobs all year: patching and touch-up painting between tenants, door hardware, caulking, blinds, shelves, assembly and minor fixes. They want someone who replies fast, shows up, finishes the work and sends a clear invoice. If you are that person, they can become repeat customers.\n\nTo approach them:\n1. Make a list of local property managers, rental agencies and small landlords. Search online, and look at the \"for rent\" signs in your area.\n2. Send a short, professional email with your job menu, the area you cover, and a line about your insurance if you have it.\n3. Offer to do one small trial job so they can see your work.\n\nExpect them to ask questions. Some will want proof of insurance. Some may ask for a tax form or business details. In some places they may need to see a license or registration. Have your answers ready, and check your local rules before you promise anything.\n\nWork habits that win this kind of customer:\n- Get a written work order or email approval before starting each job.\n- Take before and after photos.\n- Follow their rules for entering occupied units. Entry is usually arranged through the manager, with proper notice. Never enter a tenant's home on your own.\n- Invoice promptly with the property address on it.\n\nBe honest about the downsides. Some managers push hard on price, and some pay slowly, so ask about payment terms up front. Try not to let one customer become most of your work."},
  {"vid": "c51cab68", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Reply to a new inquiry within the hour", "time": "1 min", "summary": "How a quick, clear first reply wins jobs that go to the first handyman who answers properly.", "action": "Write a reply template with three questions and two visit times, and save it as a text shortcut on your phone.", "takeaway": "A fast, clear reply often decides who gets the job.", "content": "People often contact several handymen and book whoever answers first and answers clearly. Aim to reply within the hour during the day, even if only to say when you will follow up.\n\nA good first reply thanks them, asks three things (what the job is, where it is, and for a photo), and offers two times to visit or call. If the job is on your menu, you can share the price or range. If not, say you will quote after seeing the photo or visiting.\n\nDo not guess at prices for jobs you have not seen. And if the request needs a licensed trade, say so kindly and offer the name of someone to call.\n\nSet up a business voicemail greeting so callers know they reached the right place."},
  {"vid": "e7563eac", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Set up your Google Business Profile", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A free listing that helps people nearby find you when they search for a handyman, and how to keep it honest.", "action": "Start your Google Business Profile with your real name, main category, service area, phone number and three photos of finished work.", "takeaway": "Complete, honest and active profiles get found, so keep yours updated and real.", "content": "A Google Business Profile is a free listing that can appear in Google Search and Maps when people nearby look for a handyman. It shows your name, what you do, where you work, your photos, your reviews and a way to contact you.\n\nTo set it up, search for Google Business Profile and sign in with a Google account. Then:\n1. Use your real business name. Do not stuff it with extra keywords, because Google's rules do not allow that.\n2. Choose a main category that fits your work, and add the services you offer.\n3. If you travel to customers and have no shop, set it up as a service-area business and list the areas you cover. Do not list an address that is not really yours.\n4. Add your phone number, hours and a short, plain description.\n5. Add photos of finished work, with the owner's permission.\n6. Follow the steps Google uses to verify that the business is real. The method can vary.\n\nThen keep it alive. Add a new photo every week or two, reply to every review and keep your hours current. Read Google's guidelines, because they change and breaking them can get a profile suspended.\n\nNever write reviews for yourself or buy them. Be patient, too. A new profile can take weeks before it shows up well, and it works best alongside word of mouth, not instead of it.\n\nWhen customers ask how to find you, send them the profile link. It is also the link you will use to ask for reviews."},
  {"vid": "a212f8ae", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Ask for a review after every finished job", "time": "1 min", "summary": "When to ask, how to make it one tap, and how to answer reviews, including the bad ones.", "action": "Copy your Google Business Profile review link, and write the text message you will send to a happy customer after each job.", "takeaway": "Ask right after a good job, make it easy, and answer every review politely.", "content": "The best time to ask is right after you finish, while the customer is pleased and the result is in front of them. Ask in person first: \"If you're happy with the work, would you leave a short review? I'll text you the link.\"\n\nSend the link straight away, so it takes one tap. Do not offer money or discounts for a review, because review platforms usually forbid it. Do not write your own or ask friends who never used you.\n\nReply to every review. Thank people by name. If a review is negative, stay calm, say sorry they were not happy, say how you will fix it and offer to talk by phone. Future customers read your replies.\n\nWhile you are asking, ask this too: \"Do you know someone else with a to-do list?\""},
  {"vid": "ebf7f80c", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Estimate time and materials for a small job", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A step-by-step way to work out how long a job takes, what it costs and who buys the materials.", "action": "Pick one job from your menu, break it into steps, add up the minutes including travel and clean-up, and write the total.", "takeaway": "Count every step, including travel and clean-up, then add a buffer.", "content": "Underpricing is the most common mistake beginners make, and it usually happens because they only count the work itself. Use this method instead.\n\nFirst, break the job into steps and give each one a time: travel, unloading, protecting the floor, the work, cleaning up, talking to the customer and loading out. Add them up. A job that looks like thirty minutes often takes an hour or more. Then add a buffer for surprises. Some people add around a quarter, which is only an example, and you can adjust as you learn how long your own jobs run.\n\nSecond, decide how to charge. A flat price works well for jobs on your menu. An hourly rate suits open-ended work. Many handymen use both. To find a fair rate, check what handymen near you charge, then make sure yours covers tools, fuel, insurance, taxes and the unpaid time you spend quoting, driving and shopping. Prices vary a lot by area, so research yours.\n\nExample only: a TV mount might be 30 minutes of travel and set-up, 45 minutes of work and 15 minutes of clean-up and handover. That is about an hour and a half of your time, plus the bracket and any cable cover.\n\nThird, decide who buys materials. Either the customer supplies them, or you buy them and charge the receipt cost plus a small handling fee for the shopping and the risk. Tell the customer which way it works before you start, and keep receipts. Some places have rules about how materials are charged, so check yours.\n\nFinally, say whether your number is a fixed quote or a good-faith estimate, and write what is included and excluded."},
  {"vid": "286c1656", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Set a minimum charge for every visit", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Why even a ten-minute fix has a cost, and how a minimum charge keeps small calls worth your time.", "action": "Work out the real cost of one small visit in time and fuel, and set a minimum charge for it that you will put on your menu.", "takeaway": "A minimum charge covers the cost of showing up, however small the job.", "content": "A small job costs nearly as much to reach as a big one. You drive, park, unload, talk, work, clean up and drive home. Without a minimum charge, a ten-minute fix can cost you an hour or more.\n\nA minimum charge, sometimes called a call-out charge, covers that. One approach is a set fee per visit or a minimum of one hour of your rate. That is only an example, so check what is normal near you and work from your own costs.\n\nTell customers upfront, put it on your menu and in your agreement, and say what it includes. Do not apologize for it. It also nudges customers to bundle small jobs into one visit.\n\nFor estimate visits, decide whether they are free or carry a fee that is credited to the job."},
  {"vid": "11f64990", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Handle a job that grows, a dispute or a defect", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Calm steps for extra work, a disagreement over the bill, or a problem with something you did.", "action": "Write the warranty line you will put in your agreement, covering what you will fix, for how long, and what is not covered.", "takeaway": "Stay calm, go back to the written scope and photos, and fix your own mistakes fast.", "content": "Three problems come up sooner or later: the job grows, the customer disagrees, or something you did fails. Handle each calmly and you will often keep the customer.\n\nWhen a job grows, stop before you do the extra work. Explain what you found, give the extra price and time, and get a yes in writing, even a text. If the new work needs a licensed trade, say so and stop.\n\nWhen there is a disagreement, listen first. Then go back to the written scope and your photos and look at the facts together. If the mistake is yours, say so and fix it. If the customer wants more than was agreed, stay polite, explain what the agreement covered and offer to quote the extra. Do not argue in front of others, and do not abandon a job half-done unless it is unsafe. If it cannot be settled, your area may have small-claims courts, mediation services or free legal advice. Check what is available where you live.\n\nWhen something fails, go back quickly. Photograph it, work out the cause, and fix it if it was your workmanship. Fixing it fast is worth more than winning the argument.\n\nThis is where a warranty on your own work helps. Choose a period, such as 30 days, as an example, and promise to fix problems with your workmanship at no charge in that time. Say it does not cover materials, existing damage or misuse. Rules about warranties differ by place, so keep your promise small and clear, and ask a qualified person to check the wording."},
  {"vid": "019a00f4", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Batch small jobs into fewer trips", "time": "1 min", "summary": "How to group visits by area and day so you spend more time working and less time driving.", "action": "Look at your next week and group any booked jobs by area, then plan one materials trip the day before.", "takeaway": "Group jobs by area, leave gaps for overruns and buy materials in one trip.", "content": "Driving, parking and setting up are unpaid time, so every trip you save is a gain. Group jobs by area and by day. If three neighbors want work, try to do all three in one visit block.\n\nAsk customers for a list rather than one task. A \"list day\" in a neighborhood works well, and customers like having several things done at once.\n\nPlan materials in one trip the day before, using a shopping list built from your estimates. Leave gaps between jobs for overruns, and give each customer an arrival window instead of an exact time.\n\nUse a calendar app on your phone with reminders. Block time for quoting, invoicing and your books too. Two or three well-planned jobs a day beat five squeezed ones."},
  {"vid": "2b350db7", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Keep simple books and set money aside for taxes", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The few records that keep you organized, and the habit of saving for taxes from every payment.", "action": "Open a spreadsheet with two tabs, money in and money out, and enter every payment and receipt from the last month.", "takeaway": "Record every payment and cost, and move a share of each payment into savings for taxes.", "content": "Bookkeeping sounds dull, but for a handyman it is only a few habits.\n\nKeep a record of every payment you receive: the date, the customer, the job and the amount. Keep a record of every cost: materials you bought, tools, fuel, insurance, license fees and your phone. Snap a photo of each receipt as soon as you get it. A spreadsheet is enough to start. A bookkeeping app such as QuickBooks can come later if you want one.\n\nSend an invoice for every job, even when the customer pays on the spot. Put on it your business name, the date, an invoice number, the customer, a short description of the work, the amount and how to pay. You can take payments by cash, check or card, and card readers and apps such as Square or Stripe can help. They charge fees, so factor that in. A tool like Jobber can handle scheduling and invoices once you are busy.\n\nTaxes. This is not tax advice, and the rules differ by country and state. In many places, money you earn as a self-employed person is not taxed before it reaches you, and you may owe income tax and other taxes, sometimes paid in installments during the year. The safe habit is to move a share of every payment into a separate savings account the day you are paid. Ask an accountant or your tax authority what share makes sense for you, which dates matter, and whether sales tax applies to your work or materials.\n\nOnce a month, take 20 minutes to review your books. Know what came in, what went out and what is set aside."},
  {"vid": "c9aa311b", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Turn one-time customers into repeat customers", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Simple follow-ups that bring happy customers back, and a note on growing carefully.", "action": "After your next job, save the customer in your phone with notes on the work, and set a reminder to check in two weeks later.", "takeaway": "Steady work comes from people who already trust you, so keep in touch lightly.", "content": "Most of your steady work will come from people who have already used you. After each job, save the customer's contact with a note on what you did, paint colors and anything you installed. That makes later fixes easy.\n\nCheck in two weeks later: \"Hi, just checking everything is still working well.\" Twice a year, such as spring and fall, send a short note: \"Time for your seasonal to-do list? Reply with what's on yours.\" Keep it light, and stop if someone asks you to.\n\nIf you get busy, raise your prices a little before you take on more hours. If you think of hiring help, check the rules for employees and contractors where you live first."}
 ]},
{"chapter":{"id": "pets", "name": "Pet Services", "icon": "🐾", "color": "#FBBF24", "description": "Walk dogs, make drop-in visits and sit pets in their own homes, starting small in your neighborhood.", "group": "local", "breakdown": {"group": "local", "oneLiner": "Walk and care for pets while their owners are away", "cost": "Low: a few hundred dollars, mostly insurance and gear", "difficulty": "Easy to start", "pieces": [{"title": "What it is", "body": "You walk dogs, drop in to feed and care for pets, and stay at the owner's home when they are away. It is not a kennel or daycare.", "points": ["Dog walks of about 20 to 60 minutes", "Drop-in visits for food, water, play and litter", "Overnight or all-day sitting in the owner's home"]}, {"title": "Who it's for", "body": "People who are reliable, calm around animals and happy to work outdoors in any weather, in a place where many households have pets.", "points": ["Dependable and on time, even in the rain", "Calm with animals of every mood", "Comfortable being trusted with someone's home"]}, {"title": "A normal week", "body": "Short, regular blocks of walks and visits, with messages, photo updates and meet-and-greets fitted in around them.", "points": ["Busy around mornings, lunchtime and evenings", "A short photo update after each visit", "Holidays and school breaks are often the busiest"]}, {"title": "What it costs to start", "body": "Mainly insurance, a few good leashes, bags and a first aid kit, plus any business registration your area requires. Costs vary by place.", "points": ["Business insurance and any bonding", "Leashes, poop bags, towels and a first aid kit", "Business registration, if your area requires it"]}, {"title": "Tools you'll use", "body": "A phone, a calendar, a messaging app and a way to take payments, plus a free Google Business Profile so neighbors can find you.", "points": ["Google Calendar, or scheduling software like Jobber", "Square, Stripe or PayPal to get paid", "Google Business Profile and a care sheet for each pet"]}, {"title": "The hard parts", "body": "Bad weather, early starts, scared or difficult pets, last-minute cancellations, holiday crunches and the weight of being responsible for a pet.", "points": ["Full schedules on days of rain, heat or ice", "Pets who are scared, sick or hard to handle", "A slow start while you build trust and reviews"]}]}, "modules": [{"title": "Start here: your first services", "goal": "Choose your first services and area, check local rules and cover, and meet your first owner with confidence."}, {"title": "Do the work safely", "goal": "Put your rules in writing, protect keys, walk dogs safely and know what to do in an emergency."}, {"title": "Find and win customers", "goal": "Tell neighbors, vets and local groups about you, set up a free listing, send updates and ask for reviews."}, {"title": "Run it like a business", "goal": "Price your visits, plan your routes, keep simple books and set limits so the work lasts."}], "checklists": [{"title": "Pet Care Startup Checklist", "items": ["Choose one or two services and a small starting area", "Check local rules for pet care work and business registration", "Get quotes for liability, care-custody-control and bonding cover", "Write your service agreement and vet authorization form", "Make a care sheet and a meet-and-greet question list", "Buy leashes, poop bags, water, towels and a first aid kit", "Save the nearest 24-hour emergency vet in your phone", "Set up a Google Business Profile and a simple price list"]}, {"title": "Before and After Every Visit Checklist", "items": ["Read the care sheet and your notes from the last visit", "Check the weather and your route before you leave", "Confirm you have the key or code, your gear and a charged phone", "Settle the pet calmly before you open a door or clip a leash", "Do the feeding, water, medicine and litter exactly as written", "Lock up, check gates and doors, and confirm the pet is secure", "Send the owner a short update with one photo", "Note anything unusual and record the visit in your log"]}], "templates": [{"title": "Pet Care Service Agreement", "category": "Contract", "body": "Pet Care Service Agreement\n\nThis agreement is between [Your Business Name] (\"Sitter\") and [Owner Name] (\"Owner\"), starting [Start Date].\n\nPet(s): [Pet Names, species and age]\nServices: [dog walk / drop-in visit / overnight stay] on [days and times]\nPrice: $[Amount] per [visit / walk / night]. Payment is due [after each visit / weekly / on the first day] by [payment method].\n\nCancellation: Please give at least [24] hours of notice. Later cancellations are charged [fee or percentage].\nHolidays: [List dates] are charged at $[Amount] and must be booked by [date].\n\nHouse rules: [for example: one dog at a time, no off-leash walking, areas of the home the Sitter may use]\n\nKeys and access: The Owner provides [key / lockbox code / smart lock code]. The Sitter keeps keys labeled with a code, not an address, and returns them by [date].\n\nEmergencies: The Owner's phone is [number]. A second contact is [name and number]. If the Owner cannot be reached, the Sitter may take the pet to [Vet Name and phone] or [Emergency Clinic and phone]. The Owner will pay the treatment costs and agrees the Sitter may approve treatment up to $[Amount].\n\nSigned: _________________________ (Owner)      Date: _______\nSigned: _________________________ (Sitter)     Date: _______\n\nThis is a starting template, not legal advice. Have it checked for your area."}, {"title": "Meet-and-Greet Script and Care Sheet", "category": "Script", "body": "Opening: \"Thanks for having me, [Owner Name]. I'd love to meet [Pet Name] first and hear how a normal day goes. I'll ask a few questions and take notes.\"\n\nLet [Pet Name] come to you. Do not reach out or crowd the pet.\n\nCare sheet questions (write the answers here):\nPet: [Name, age, breed, microchip or tag details]\nFood: [what, how much, when, treats, anything forbidden]\nMedicine and health: [name, dose, how given, known problems]\nVet and emergency clinic: [names and phone numbers]\nFears and triggers: [for example: thunder, strangers, other dogs]\nLeash and door behavior: [pulls, bolts, jumps, calm]\nWhere things are: [leash, bags, food, cleaning supplies, litter]\nHouse notes: [alarm, gate, doors, Wi-Fi if needed]\nUpdates: [text or app, how often, preferred times]\nOther contact if the Owner cannot be reached: [name and number]\n\nClosing: \"Thank you. I'll send my agreement and vet authorization form today. If you'd like to go ahead, tell me your first date and I'll put it in my calendar.\"\n\nAfter you leave, write down your own notes: [How did the pet react? Any safety concerns? Is this a good fit?]"}], "videos": [], "tools": {"type": "local", "panels": ["jobs", "estimates", "maintenance", "supplies", "reviews"]}},
 "guides":[
  {"vid": "8bbc3bda", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "What dog walking, drop-ins and pet sitting really involve", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The three core services, what a normal day looks like and why this work is harder than it sounds.", "action": "Write the three services on a page and mark the one that best fits your schedule and your comfort with animals.", "takeaway": "You are paid to keep someone's animal safe and calm, and the walk is only part of it.", "content": "Pet care work means looking after other people's animals while they are at work or away. Most beginners start with three services. A dog walk is a set stretch of time, often 20 to 60 minutes, when you take one or more dogs out. A drop-in visit is a short stop at the home to feed the pet, give fresh water, clean a litter box, play and let a dog out. In-home pet sitting means you stay at the owner's home overnight, or for a full day, so the pet keeps its normal routine. This chapter does not cover running a kennel or daycare at your own place. That is a different business with different rules.\n\nA normal day is more than the fun part. You will pick up poop in the rain. You will move between homes on a tight schedule. You will deal with dogs that pull, cats that hide and owners who text you at 9 pm. Some weeks are busy and some are quiet, and holidays are often the hardest time, because everyone travels at once.\n\nWhat owners are really buying is peace of mind. They hand you a key, an animal they love and access to their home. So the job rests on trust, good habits and clear communication more than on a love of animals alone. Loving animals helps. Being reliable, calm and careful matters more.\n\nYou do not need special training to begin, but you do need to learn safe handling, how to keep a home secure and what to do in an emergency. Those come later in this chapter. For now, ask yourself one honest question: which of these jobs would you still be happy to do three times a week in bad weather? That is the work."},
  {"vid": "a2644d61", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Start with one or two services in a small area", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Why a short menu and a tight starting area make you reliable, rested and easy to recommend.", "action": "Draw a circle on a map around your home that you can cross in ten minutes, and pick the one or two services you will offer inside it.", "takeaway": "A small area and a short menu keep you on time and easy to recommend.", "content": "It is tempting to say yes to everything, including long drives across town. Resist that at the start. Pick one or two services, such as weekday dog walks plus drop-in visits, and keep to an area you can cross in about ten minutes. Short travel time means more of your day is paid work and less of it is spent in traffic. It also means you can reach a pet quickly if something goes wrong.\n\nA short menu helps you get good. You learn the local dogs, the best routes and the gates that stick. Customers also remember you more easily. \"She walks dogs on our street\" travels further by word of mouth than a long list of services. You can add overnight sitting or other animals later, once your safety habits are solid."},
  {"vid": "aa004355", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Check your local rules and register simply", "time": "3 min", "summary": "The questions to ask your city, county or state before your first paid job, and why to avoid boarding at first.", "action": "Search your city or county website for pet sitter and dog walker rules, and write down the name and number of the office to call to confirm.", "takeaway": "Rules differ by place, so check before you start and stay with walks and visits at first.", "content": "Rules for pet care are not the same everywhere. Some places ask nothing of a dog walker beyond an ordinary business registration. Others have extra rules for walkers, for sitters, or for anyone who looks after animals for pay. Boarding and daycare, where animals come to your own place, are far more likely to need a license, inspections or special zoning. That is one reason to begin with walks and visits at the owner's home. This course cannot tell you what your area requires, so you need to check your local rules yourself.\n\nHere is a simple way to do that:\n1. Search your city or county website for \"pet sitter\", \"dog walker\" and \"business license\".\n2. Look up how to register a small business in your state or country, and whether a business name has to be filed if it is not your own name.\n3. Ask whether there are limits on how many dogs one person may walk at once, or permits for using parks and trails commercially.\n4. If you rent or live in a condo, read your lease and building rules before running any part of the business from home.\n5. Call and ask. Most local offices will answer a short question. Write down who you spoke to and when.\n\nMany people start as a sole proprietor, which is the simplest way to work alone, but the right setup depends on where you live and your own situation. A local small business advisor, an accountant or a lawyer can tell you which fits. In the US, a Small Business Development Center is a good place to start. Checking now costs very little compared with a fine, or a business shut down later."},
  {"vid": "8e14c156", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Ask an insurance agent about pet care cover", "time": "1 min", "summary": "The questions to ask about liability, care, custody and control cover, and bonding before your first pet.", "action": "Call one independent insurance agent, ask for a quote for dog walking and pet sitting cover, and write down what it leaves out.", "takeaway": "Get covered before the first paid job, and read what the policy leaves out.", "content": "Before you take a pet into your care, talk to an insurance agent who knows small businesses. Ask about three things. First, general liability, which can help if someone is hurt or property is damaged because of your work. Second, whether the policy covers animals in your care, custody or control. Many general policies leave out injury or loss to the animal itself, so this cover often has to be added on purpose. Third, bonding, a separate protection that can reimburse a client if something goes missing from their home. Many owners like to see it.\n\nAsk what is excluded, what the deductible is, and whether walks, car trips and overnight stays are all covered. Rules and prices differ by insurer and by place, so collect at least two quotes."},
  {"vid": "9827dd9e", "mod": 0, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Run a meet-and-greet that earns the job", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A short first visit where the owner and the pet meet you, and you decide whether the job is a good fit.", "action": "Write ten questions to ask at a meet-and-greet, including one about anything that scares or upsets the pet.", "takeaway": "A meet-and-greet is an interview in both directions, so use it to say yes or no.", "content": "Before you take a pet on, ask for a meet-and-greet at the owner's home. Plan on 20 to 30 minutes. Most owners will not hand over a key until they have met you, and you should not accept one until you have met the pet.\n\nGreet the owner first, not the dog. Let the pet come to you and do not rush to pet it. Many dogs are wary of strangers, and cats may hide. A pet who cannot settle around you now may not be a good fit for a visit when the owner is away.\n\nThen listen and look. Ask about:\n1. Feeding times, amounts, treats and anything the pet must never eat\n2. Medicines, health problems, and the name and number of the vet\n3. Fears, such as thunder, other dogs, visitors or the vacuum cleaner\n4. Behavior on the leash, at the door and around strangers\n5. Where the leash, bags, food and cleaning supplies are kept\n6. How the owner wants updates, and how often\n\nWalk through the parts of the home you will use and note anything risky, such as a gate that does not latch, a door that opens to the street, or the place the cat hides. Ask the owner before taking any photos.\n\nEnd by saying what happens next. Offer to send your service agreement and a care sheet to fill in. Also remember that you can say no. A dog that has bitten someone, or a home where you feel uneasy, is a fair reason to pass. You are choosing clients too."},
  {"vid": "043d6519", "mod": 0, "kind": "short", "title": "Fill in a care sheet for every pet", "time": "1 min", "summary": "One page per pet with food, medicine, habits, fears and vet details, kept where you can find it fast.", "action": "Make a one-page care sheet with headings for food, medicine, habits and contacts, and use it as your master copy for owners to fill in.", "takeaway": "A written care sheet keeps every visit consistent, even if someone else has to cover for you.", "content": "A care sheet is one page per pet, filled in by the owner and checked by you at the meet-and-greet. It saves you from relying on memory.\n\nInclude the pet's name, age and breed, a recent photo, and microchip or tag details. Add feeding times and amounts, medicines and how they are given, and any health problems. List the vet's name and number, plus an emergency clinic nearby. Note habits and fears, like pulling on the leash or hiding when the doorbell rings. Write down where supplies are kept, door and alarm instructions, and a backup contact.\n\nKeep the sheets on your phone, protected by a passcode. Ask owners to update the sheet whenever something changes, and collect only the personal details you need."},
  {"vid": "4cc93517", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Put your rules and service agreement in writing", "time": "3 min", "summary": "What a simple service agreement covers, so both sides know the price, the schedule, the rules and who to call.", "action": "Fill in the service agreement template from this chapter with your own details, then read it out loud to see if it makes sense.", "takeaway": "A short written agreement prevents most arguments before they start.", "content": "A handshake is friendly, but a short written agreement protects you and the owner. It does not have to be long. One or two pages in plain words is enough, and you can send it by email to be signed on a phone.\n\nA good agreement covers:\n1. Who you and the owner are, with phone numbers and a second emergency contact\n2. Which pets and which services, on which days and at what times\n3. The price, how and when you are paid, and what happens if payment is late\n4. Your cancellation and holiday policy\n5. House rules, such as no off-leash walking and which parts of the home you will use\n6. Permission to take the pet to a vet in an emergency, and who pays\n7. How you will handle keys and alarm codes\n8. What happens if you cannot make a visit, such as a backup sitter the owner knows about\n\nClear rules are not rude. If you only walk dogs one at a time, or you do not give injections, or you do not visit dog parks, say so now. It is far easier to explain at the start than in the middle of a problem.\n\nKeep a signed copy for every client in a safe place, and ask each owner to read it before your first visit and to tell you if anything is unclear.\n\nContracts and what they must include differ by place. Have your template checked by a local lawyer or a small business advisor before you rely on it."},
  {"vid": "2ac6c09a", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Treat house keys like cash", "time": "1 min", "summary": "How to receive, label, store and return keys and codes so a lost key never turns into a worse problem.", "action": "Decide where you will store keys, such as a locked box, and write down a rule that no key will ever carry an address.", "takeaway": "Never label a key with an address, and always know where every key is.", "content": "Owners give you access to their home. Treat every key as if it were cash.\n\nNever write an address or a name on a key. Use a code such as A12, and keep the list that matches codes to clients somewhere else. Store keys in a locked box at home, or on your body while you work. Never leave them loose in a bag or in your car.\n\nAsk whether a lockbox or smart lock code would be simpler, since codes can be changed later. Never lend a key or code to anyone else. If a key is lost, tell the owner straight away and plainly, and tell your insurer. At the end of the job, return the key and ask the owner to confirm in writing that they have it."},
  {"vid": "e8d41007", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Walk dogs safely: leashes, gear and weather", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Leash habits, gear and weather checks that keep dogs and walkers out of trouble.", "action": "Check your kit for sturdy leashes, a spare, poop bags, water and a small first aid kit, and buy anything that is missing.", "takeaway": "Keep every dog on a leash and under control, and check the weather before you leave.", "content": "Most walking accidents happen in a few predictable moments: at a door, at a street crossing, when another dog appears, or when a dog is startled. A few firm habits cover most of them.\n\nKeep the dog on a leash anywhere outside a private, fenced yard. Leashes slip out of hands, so loop the handle around your wrist or hold it with both hands when a dog pulls. A dog that can slip out of its collar should wear a well-fitted harness. Many walkers add a second point of connection when the owner agrees. Be careful with retractable leashes in busy places, because they give you little control. Carry a spare leash in case yours breaks.\n\nAt front doors, gates and car doors, stop and settle the dog before you open anything. Many escapes happen in the first ten seconds.\n\nCheck the weather before every walk. In hot weather, test the pavement with the back of your hand, shorten the walk and bring water. In cold weather, ice and salt can hurt paws, and some dogs chill quickly. Many dogs are frightened by thunder, so skip or shorten walks in storms.\n\nCarry poop bags and pick up after every dog. Keep your phone charged, and keep your attention on the dog and not the screen.\n\nRules on leashes, how many dogs you can walk and where dogs may go differ by place, so check your local rules. When in doubt, walk fewer dogs at a time."},
  {"vid": "a800e5c0", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Read dog body language and never walk dogs that might fight", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Warning signs to learn, and why a dog with a history of fighting walks alone or not at all.", "action": "Write down five warning signs, such as a stiff body, a hard stare and raised hair, and watch for them on your next walk.", "takeaway": "When a dog gives warning signs, add distance. If it has fought before, walk it alone.", "content": "Dogs tell you how they feel with their whole body. A relaxed dog has a loose body, soft eyes and a tail that wags freely. Warning signs include a stiff body, a hard stare, raised hair on the back, a tail held high and stiff, lips pulled back, freezing or growling. Stress can look like yawning, lip licking or turning away. A wagging tail does not always mean friendly.\n\nWhen you see warning signs, stay calm, add distance and turn away without yanking the leash. Do not reach over or pet a stressed dog.\n\nWalk dogs together only if the owners agree and you have seen the dogs get along. If a dog has ever bitten or fought another dog, it walks alone, or you decline the job. If you are unsure, say no."},
  {"vid": "5976ac24", "mod": 1, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Make an emergency plan before you need one", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A vet authorization form, emergency contacts and a plan for a lost or hurt pet, all written before day one.", "action": "Find the nearest 24-hour emergency vet to your service area and save its name, number and address in your phone.", "takeaway": "Know who to call and what the owner has agreed to before anything goes wrong.", "content": "Emergencies are rare, but sooner or later you will face one, and the worst time to plan is in the middle of it. Do three things in advance.\n\nFirst, get a vet authorization form signed by every client. It names the pet's regular vet and a backup emergency clinic, says how much the owner is willing to spend before you must reach them, and gives you permission to get the pet treated if the owner cannot be reached. Include the owner's number and a second contact.\n\nSecond, know the closest emergency vet to each area you work in. Save the address and number in your phone, and know how you would get there.\n\nThird, write a lost-pet plan. If a pet slips out, call the owner first, then search the nearby area calmly and ask neighbors to help. Ask owners for a recent photo and microchip details ahead of time. Stay in touch with the owner until the pet is found.\n\nIf a pet is hurt or acting strangely, do not try to diagnose it. Pet first aid is about keeping the animal safe and calm until a vet takes over. A pet first aid course from a recognized provider will teach you what to do about things like choking, bleeding and heat stress. You are not a vet. Never give medicine the owner has not told you about. Call the vet or an animal poison hotline for advice, and call the owner.\n\nAfterward, write down what happened, what you did and when, and tell your insurer if there was an injury."},
  {"vid": "ddbccadc", "mod": 1, "kind": "short", "title": "Take extra care with puppies, seniors, cats and small animals", "time": "1 min", "summary": "How needs change for young, old and small pets, and when to say you are not the right sitter.", "action": "For each kind of animal you plan to care for, write down one thing you need to learn before accepting the job.", "takeaway": "Young, old and small pets need extra care, so only take what you know how to do.", "content": "Not every pet is an adult dog with a normal routine. Puppies need frequent outings and may not have finished their vaccines, so ask the owner what the vet says about where they can go. Senior dogs may be stiff, tire quickly and need medicine on a schedule, so shorten walks and watch for limping or heavy breathing. Cats often hide, so find the cat at every visit and notice how it eats, drinks and uses the litter box. Small animals such as rabbits, guinea pigs and birds can be fragile and have special food and temperature needs.\n\nAsk for written instructions for every special case. If you feel out of your depth, decline politely or suggest someone with the right experience. That is better than an accident."},
  {"vid": "a5e29ff8", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Find your first customers close to home", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Who to tell first, what to say, and how to use word of mouth, flyers and pet care apps.", "action": "Message ten people who live near you with what you offer, the area you cover and the fact that you are taking your first few clients.", "takeaway": "Your first clients will almost always come from people who already know you or see you around.", "content": "Your first customers rarely come from advertising. They come from people who know someone with a dog or a cat. Start by telling everyone you know, in plain words: what you do, where, and that you are taking your first few clients.\n\nWalk your own neighborhood. You will notice the same dogs out at the same hours, which hints at who might want help. Be friendly, but do not hand out cards while someone is trying to manage their dog. Ask if they would like to hear about your service, and leave a card if they say yes. Put a simple flyer on community boards that allow them, like coffee shops, apartment lobbies and pet stores, and always ask first.\n\nSome people also use pet care apps such as Rover or Wag. They can bring customers, but they take a fee and set their own rules, so read the current terms before deciding if they suit you.\n\nOffer something small and easy to say yes to, like a free meet-and-greet or a discounted first walk. Do a great job for your first few clients, then ask whether they know anyone who would like the same. A neighbor who trusts you will tell the next one.\n\nExpect a slow start. A few weeks with no calls does not mean you have failed. It means you should talk to more people, keep your area small and keep your offer clear. Never post photos of clients' pets or homes without their permission."},
  {"vid": "97137492", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Introduce yourself to local vets and groomers", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Vet offices and groomers meet pet owners all day, so a friendly hello can lead to steady referrals.", "action": "Make a list of three vets or groomers near you and drop off your card with a one-line introduction.", "takeaway": "Vets and groomers are trusted by owners, so earn their trust and they may pass your name along.", "content": "Vet clinics, groomers, trainers and pet supply stores talk to your future customers every day. Make a short list of the ones near you and visit at a quiet time, not on a busy Saturday. Say who you are, what you offer and which neighborhoods you cover. Bring a few business cards and ask if you could leave them or put one on a community board.\n\nDo not ask for referrals on the first visit. Be useful instead. Ask what their clients most often need, and be clear about what you do not do. They will only recommend someone they trust, so being polite and reliable is what counts. Do not offer money for referrals, since a thank-you note is plenty. If a business sends you a client, tell them the visit went well."},
  {"vid": "ff5a7fe9", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Set up a free Google Business Profile", "time": "3 min", "summary": "How to claim a listing so people searching for a pet sitter near them can find and contact you.", "action": "Create your Google Business Profile, add your service area and hours, and upload two clear photos of yourself and your gear.", "takeaway": "A complete Google profile helps nearby owners find you at the moment they search.", "content": "When someone types \"dog walker near me\", Google shows a short list of local businesses. A free Google Business Profile is how you try to appear there. It cannot promise you a spot, but without one you are much harder to find.\n\nSetting it up usually takes under an hour:\n1. Go to Google Business Profile and sign in with a Google account, ideally one made for the business.\n2. Enter your business name, the same one you use on cards and in person.\n3. Choose a category that fits, such as dog walker or pet sitter, from the list Google offers.\n4. Because you go to the customer's home, choose the option for a business that serves customers at their location, and enter the neighborhoods or ZIP codes you cover. You can keep your home address hidden.\n5. Add a phone number, hours, a short description and your services.\n6. Verify the business the way Google asks. The method varies and can take a few days.\n\nThen add a few photos, such as you with your gear, or a dog you walk with the owner's permission. Use the same name, phone number and area everywhere online so people can trust that you are real.\n\nKeep the profile current. Update your hours when they change, post a short update now and then and reply politely to reviews. Google changes its rules and screens from time to time, so follow its on-screen steps. Also read its guidance on business names, because adding extra words to a name can get a profile suspended."},
  {"vid": "c9271106", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Use Nextdoor and local groups without spamming", "time": "1 min", "summary": "How to introduce yourself in neighborhood apps and groups in a friendly way that follows each group's rules.", "action": "Join one neighborhood group, read its rules, and write a short friendly introduction you could post if business posts are allowed.", "takeaway": "Be a helpful neighbor first, and follow each group's rules about advertising.", "content": "Neighborhood apps like Nextdoor, local Facebook groups and community chat groups are where neighbors ask, \"Does anyone know a good dog walker?\" You want to be the name someone else gives. Join first and read the rules, because many groups limit business posts to a certain day or section. Break those rules and you can be removed.\n\nWhen it is allowed, post one short, friendly introduction: your first name, what you offer, the streets you cover and that you offer a free meet-and-greet. Skip the long ad. Answer people when they ask for recommendations, and be honest about what you do not offer. Never criticize other sitters. Never share where anyone lives or when they are away, and never post a client's pet or home without clear permission."},
  {"vid": "8a2f7379", "mod": 2, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Send photo and message updates owners love", "time": "3 min", "summary": "A simple update routine that reassures owners while they are away and gives them a reason to recommend you.", "action": "Write the three-line message you will send after every visit, and decide on the one photo you will always include.", "takeaway": "A short, honest update after every visit is some of the best marketing you will do.", "content": "Owners hand over their pet and then spend the day wondering how it is going. A short update after every visit takes the worry away. It is also what makes people say, \"You have to try her.\"\n\nAgree how updates will be sent at the meet-and-greet: a text message, a messaging app, or a client app if you use one. Ask how often they want one. Many owners want one after every walk or visit.\n\nA good update is short and real. For example: \"Rosie had a good 30-minute walk, ate all her dinner and drank water. She loved the park by the library. See you tomorrow at noon.\" Add one clear photo of the pet looking relaxed. Take it after the care is done, not before.\n\nBe honest. If a dog skipped dinner, a cat hid for the whole visit or a dog had an upset stomach, say so plainly and say what you did. Owners trust honesty, and hiding a problem to look good costs far more trust than reporting it. For anything urgent, call instead of texting.\n\nRespect the owner's privacy. The photos of their home and pet belong to them. Ask before using any on social media or your profile, and never show the inside of a home, a house number or a key.\n\nFinally, do not let updates take time away from the pet. Send them when you have finished and are safely out of the door, never while you are driving. A good update takes less than a minute."},
  {"vid": "1a1fc120", "mod": 2, "kind": "short", "title": "Ask for reviews at the right moment", "time": "1 min", "summary": "When and how to ask happy clients for a short honest review, and how to reply to ones you do not love.", "action": "Pick your three happiest clients and send each a short message asking for a review, with a direct link to your profile.", "takeaway": "Ask soon after a great visit, make it easy, and always reply politely.", "content": "Most new owners choose a sitter by reading reviews, and most happy clients never leave one unless asked. The best time to ask is right after a good moment, like when an owner has thanked you for a great update or come home to a relaxed pet.\n\nMake it easy. Send the direct link to your Google Business Profile and say, \"If you have a minute, a short honest review would really help a small business like mine.\" Ask for honest feedback, not for a certain score. Do not offer gifts or discounts in return, and never write or buy your own, since platforms ban it and some places have rules against it.\n\nReply to every review. If a review is critical, wait a day, stay polite and factual, and offer to talk offline."},
  {"vid": "cced8819", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Price walks and visits by length of time", "time": "3 min", "summary": "How to set prices by visit length, cover your costs and check them against what is normal near you.", "action": "Look up three local walkers or sitters, note their prices by visit length, and draft your own price list with three lengths.", "takeaway": "Price by visit length, include your costs, and check what is normal in your area.", "content": "Most pet care is priced by the visit. A short drop-in might be 15 to 20 minutes, a standard walk 30 minutes and a longer walk 45 or 60 minutes. Overnight sitting is usually priced per night. Pricing by visit length is simple for the owner to understand and easy for you to plan around.\n\nHere is one way to find your numbers. First, look at what walkers and sitters near you charge. Their websites and local groups will show you. Prices vary a lot by area and by service, so what you see where you live matters more than any number in a course. Second, work out your costs, including insurance, supplies, phone, fuel or transit, and the unpaid minutes spent traveling between homes. Third, count the full time a visit takes: arriving, greeting the pet, cleaning up and sending the update.\n\nAs a pure example, a 30-minute walk might be priced anywhere from the low twenties to the thirties in dollars, depending on where you live. Treat that as a rough guess, not a rule.\n\nThen decide on extras. You might charge more for a second pet, for giving medicine, for a puppy, for last-minute bookings and for trips outside your usual area. List them on a simple price sheet so there are no surprises.\n\nDo not start with the lowest price in town just to win clients. It tends to attract people who leave over small differences, and it is hard to raise later. Start with a fair price, review your costs after a month or two, and raise prices gently for new clients when you need to."},
  {"vid": "d719261a", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Set a holiday and cancellation policy", "time": "1 min", "summary": "A simple written policy for holidays, late cancellations and no-shows that you can apply kindly.", "action": "Write your cancellation rule in two sentences, including how much notice you need, and add it to your service agreement.", "takeaway": "Decide your holiday and cancellation rules in advance and put them in the agreement.", "content": "When you hold a time slot, you turn down other work, so late cancellations cost you. Write the rule before it happens. Many sitters ask for 24 hours of notice and charge a fee for late cancellations. Choose what feels fair in your area.\n\nHolidays are often your busiest and hardest time, because everyone travels at once. Many sitters charge a higher rate on major holidays and ask owners to book early. Decide which days count, how far ahead you need bookings, and how many holiday clients you can handle. Tell clients early.\n\nBe kind but consistent. You can waive a fee in a real emergency, but make it your choice and not a habit. Having the rule in your agreement means you never have to argue about it."},
  {"vid": "ff046bb4", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Plan routes and time blocks that actually work", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Batch visits by area, leave buffers and build a weekly schedule you can keep.", "action": "Draw a weekly grid, block out your walk and visit times, and leave a ten-minute buffer after every visit.", "takeaway": "Group visits by area, build in buffers and never fill every minute of the day.", "content": "Your schedule is your product. If you are late, rushed or worn out, the pets and owners feel it. A few simple habits help.\n\nStart with demand. Many dog owners want midday walks, so weekday lunch hours tend to fill first. Mornings, evenings and holidays are busy too. Decide the blocks you can offer reliably and treat the rest as time off. Write them down as fixed blocks, such as a morning visit block, a midday walk block and an afternoon block.\n\nThen batch by area. Put clients who live on the same streets into the same block, so you spend minutes, not half an hour, getting from one to the next. Open a map and look at your clients as dots. If two clients are far apart, they cannot both have a noon walk.\n\nLeave buffers. A 30-minute walk often takes closer to 45 minutes once you add arriving, greeting, cleaning up, writing the update and moving on. Add ten minutes after each visit for the unexpected: a dog who will not come in, a cat who hides, a parking space that is gone. Buffers protect your reputation.\n\nUse one calendar for everything, such as Google Calendar, with each visit's address and a note about the key. Some people move to scheduling software like Jobber once they have enough clients, but a calendar and a spreadsheet are plenty at first.\n\nPlan a backup. Tell a trusted friend or fellow sitter that you may need cover if you get sick, and tell clients your plan in the agreement.\n\nFinally, say no to clients who do not fit your blocks. A packed, rushed day is the first step toward mistakes."},
  {"vid": "a0376c9b", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Handle a difficult client or a hard goodbye", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Calm ways to handle a demanding owner, a client who leaves, or the loss of a pet you cared for.", "action": "Write a short, kind message for an upset owner, and a second one you could send when a client's pet has died.", "takeaway": "Stay calm, point back to the agreement, and be kind when things end.", "content": "Most owners are lovely, but some will be demanding, late to pay or always changing plans. Stay calm and reply in writing. Point back to the agreement: \"As we agreed, cancellations with less than 24 hours of notice are charged.\" Offer one clear fix. If it keeps happening, it is fine to end the relationship politely. Suggest another sitter.\n\nHard goodbyes are part of this work. Pets get old and sick, and clients' pets will die. If that happens, contact the owner gently, say you are sorry, and leave business out of it. A short card means a lot. If a pet is lost or dies while in your care, call the owner right away, tell the truth and tell your insurer. Look after yourself too. It is normal to feel sad."},
  {"vid": "2efb73a3", "mod": 3, "kind": "lesson", "title": "Keep simple books and set money aside for taxes", "time": "3 min", "summary": "Track what comes in and goes out, keep receipts, and set money aside for taxes with a professional's help.", "action": "Open a separate bank account for the business and start a simple sheet with columns for date, client, amount and expense.", "takeaway": "Keep business money separate, record everything and ask a professional how much to set aside.", "content": "Money is easy to lose track of when it arrives in small amounts from many clients. A simple system keeps you calm at tax time.\n\nFirst, open a separate bank account for the business, even a basic one. Run every payment and expense through it. Keeping personal and business money apart is the single best habit for clean books.\n\nSecond, record every payment and every cost. A spreadsheet with the date, client, service, amount and payment method is enough at first. As you grow, you might move to bookkeeping software like QuickBooks, and payment tools such as Square, Stripe or PayPal keep records of what you were paid. Keep receipts for things like insurance, leashes, bags, mileage and your phone plan. Take a photo of each receipt on the day.\n\nThird, set money aside for taxes. When you are self-employed, nobody takes tax out of your payments, and the bill can come as a surprise. Many people move a fixed share of every payment into a separate savings account. What share is right depends on where you live and your whole situation, so ask an accountant or your tax office. Also ask whether you need to make payments during the year.\n\nFourth, find out which expenses and records count for tax where you live. This course cannot tell you, and rules differ by country and state. A qualified tax professional can, and an hour of their time early on can save a lot of trouble.\n\nReview your books for ten minutes every week. Chase late payments, check that everything is recorded and note what you spent. Ask your accountant how long you need to keep your records."},
  {"vid": "2483d9a9", "mod": 3, "kind": "short", "title": "Protect your time and avoid burnout", "time": "1 min", "summary": "Set working hours, days off and a limit on clients so the work stays enjoyable.", "action": "Pick one day or half day each week as time off, and put it in your calendar and your client agreement.", "takeaway": "Set limits early, because a rested sitter is a safer and kinder sitter.", "content": "Pet care can quietly take over your life. Clients text at night, holidays disappear and the weather is never on your side. Burnout is a real risk, and tired people make mistakes with animals and keys.\n\nSet working hours and tell clients when you reply to messages. Pick a regular day off and protect it. Decide how many clients, and how many dogs on one walk, is your limit. Say no when you are full, or keep a short waiting list. Charge fairly so you do not need to take every job, and arrange a backup sitter for sick days. If the work stops being enjoyable, change your schedule, raise your prices or let go of the clients who drain you."}
 ]}
]);
